WEBVTT

00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:03.450
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是《美国生活》。

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:07.490
<v Ira Glass>[DOOR OPENING]
<v Ira Glass>[门开]

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:13.220
<v Navyn Salem>OK, let's see what we got in here. How are you? Good.
<v Navyn Salem>好，让我们看看这里有什么。你好吗？很好。

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:15.020
<v Ira Glass>This is huge.
<v Ira Glass>这太宏大了。

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:27.690
<v Ira Glass>We're in a warehouse in Rhode Island, whose floor space is bigger than two full football fields, three stories tall, row after row after row of cardboard boxes stacked high, stretching far into the distance.
<v Ira Glass>我们在罗得岛州的一个仓库里，这里的地板面积比两个完整的足球场还要大，三层楼高，成排成排的高高堆叠的纸箱一直延伸到远方。

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:32.400
<v Ira Glass>I feel like the only thing that I know to compare this to is the last scene of Raiders of the Lost Ark.
<v Ira Glass>我觉得我唯一能拿来做类比的，只有《夺宝奇兵》的最后那一幕。

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.380
<v Sarah Rumsey>[LAUGHS] That's a pretty good comparison.
<v Sarah Rumsey>[笑] 这个比方挺贴切的。

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:56.790
<v Ira Glass>Most of these cardboard boxes contain this nutritional peanut paste. It's a kind of miracle food made to strict international standards, officially called RUTF, Ready to Use Therapeutic Food, or Plumpy'Nut. Each box has two months' worth, enough to save the life of one severely malnourished child.
<v Ira Glass>这些纸箱里大部分装的是这种营养花生酱。这是一种按照严格国际标准制造的奇迹食品，官方称为RUTF，即即食治疗性食品，也就是Plumpy'Nut。每箱够用两个月，足以拯救一个严重营养不良的儿童的生命。

00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:00.460
<v Sarah Rumsey>Over 200,000 boxes that are sitting here in this warehouse.
<v Sarah Rumsey>这个仓库里堆放着超过20万箱。

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:04.930
<v Navyn Salem>Each one represents one child's life.
<v Navyn Salem>每一箱都代表着一个孩子的生命。

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:23.520
<v Ira Glass>Navyn Salem runs this place, making this stuff. And Sarah Rumsey is one of her managers. They explained that we're looking at millions of dollars of aid that's already been paid for by the United States government. They manufactured it for the US Agency for International Development, USAID, before it was shut down by Elon Musk and Donald Trump.
<v Ira Glass>纳温·萨勒姆（Navyn Salem）经营着这个地方，制造这些东西。莎拉·拉姆西（Sarah Rumsey）是她的经理之一。她们解释说，我们看到的是价值数百万美元的援助，这些援助已经由美国政府支付了费用。这是她们在被埃隆·马斯克和唐纳德·特朗普关闭之前，为美国国际开发署（USAID）制造的。

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:52.780
<v Navyn Salem>If you look at these boxes, the majority do not have a country, destination, or paperwork to leave the facility. And it's been sitting here since February, waiting for paperwork to be signed. The problem when you dismantle all of USAID, there are no people who can proceed with basic day-to-day jobs to process basic things, like contracts.
<v Navyn Salem>如果你看看这些箱子，大多数都没有国家、目的地或离开设施的文件。而且它从二月份就一直放在这里，等待文件签署。拆散整个美国国际开发署的问题在于，没有可以推进日常基本工作来处理合同等基本事务的人。

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:54.760
<v Ira Glass>And where was this food supposed to go? Do you know?
<v Ira Glass>这些食物原本应该送到哪里去？你知道吗？

00:01:54.760 --> 00:02:03.730
<v Navyn Salem>So this would typically go to Mali, Chad, Democratic Republic of the Congo, Sudan, South Sudan.
<v Navyn Salem>所以这些通常会运往马里、乍得、刚果民主共和国、苏丹、南苏丹。

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:19.300
<v Ira Glass>Nearly a half billion dollars' worth of food was stuck in warehouses like this one or was in transit right after President Trump froze all foreign aid so suddenly, his first day in office. It really wasn't clear what was going to happen to it, if it was going to be left to spoil, if it would get delivered.
<v Ira Glass>就在特朗普总统上任第一天突然冻结所有对外援助后，近五亿美元的食品就被困在像这样的仓库里或在运输途中。当时真的不清楚这些东西会怎么样，是会被留着变质，还是会送达。

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:46.280
<v Ira Glass>Then the State Department said that life-saving food and medical aid could get permission to continue, but lots of it stayed stuck. Things were in a weird limbo while they were dismantling the agency that did that work, USAID, and transferring any of USAID's remaining functions over to the State Department. People like Navyn had to operate in that limbo, in her case, trying to get this product out of her warehouse to the people who need it.
<v Ira Glass>随后，国务院表示，拯救生命的食品和医疗援助可以获得继续进行的许可，但其中许多物资仍然滞留。在拆除负责这项工作的机构——美国国际开发署，并将美国国际开发署的任何剩余职能移交给国务院的过程中，一切都处于一种奇怪的悬而未决的状态。像纳温这样的人必须在这种不确定状态下运营，对她来说，就是试图将这批产品从她的仓库运送到需要它的人手中。

00:02:46.280 --> 00:03:00.470
<v Navyn Salem>I feel like I'm in a movie that I don't want to be in. I don't sleep. I get up at 5 o'clock in the morning every day. The money and the time that we're spending trying to get a single signature--
<v Navyn Salem>我觉得我身处一部我不想参演的电影里。我睡不着觉。我每天早上5点起床。我们为了争取一个签字而花费的金钱和时间——

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:24.570
<v Ira Glass>Navyn founded this business. It's called Edesia. It's a nonprofit. We sit on the concrete floor in the middle of all these boxes to do our interview. And every detail that she tells me about how she ended up starting this company sounds like it's part of a fable in a kid's storybook, but a weird storybook where the hero dreams not of being a princess or a scientist or a detective, but of being a cog in America's foreign aid establishment.
<v Ira Glass>纳温创立了这家企业。它叫埃迪西亚（Edesia）。这是一个非营利组织。我们在所有这些箱子中间的水泥地板上坐下来进行采访。她向我讲述的关于她如何创办这家公司的每一个细节，听起来都像是一个儿童故事书里的寓言，但这是一本奇怪的故事书，故事里的英雄梦想不是成为公主、科学家或侦探，而是成为美国对外援助建制中的一颗螺丝钉。

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:54.770
<v Navyn Salem>The reason that I'm here is my dad was given the first scholarship ever to an African student by USAID. He came to the United States, lived with a host family, and lived in Michigan and went to university in Michigan. I was born here. And when I wrote my first grant proposal to USAID, I wrote a cover letter, and I said, I'm here because of you.
<v Navyn Salem>我之所以来这里，是因为我父亲获得了美国国际开发署颁发给非洲学生的第一个奖学金。他来到美国，和一个接待家庭一起生活在密歇根州，并在密歇根州上大学。我出生在这里。当我向美国国际开发署写第一份赠款申请书时，我写了一封求职信，我说，我来到这里是因为你们。

00:03:54.770 --> 00:04:15.920
<v Ira Glass>Her dad came from Tanzania. And when her own kids were preschool age-- she has four girls-- Navyn says she started thinking a lot about doing something to help parents back in Tanzania and places like it. She heard about RUTFs from a story she saw on 60 Minutes and in 2008, started manufacturing, first in Tanzania, then in Rhode Island.
<v Ira Glass>她的父亲来自坦桑尼亚。当她自己的孩子还是学龄前儿童时——她有四个女孩——纳温说她开始花很多心思去做一些事情来帮助坦桑尼亚及其类似地方的父母。她从《60分钟》节目的一则报道中听说了RUTF，并于2008年开始生产，最初在坦桑尼亚，后来在罗得岛州。

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:40.350
<v Ira Glass>She had no manufacturing experience at all, had to learn everything, and then grew the business to a factory with 150 employees, a big chunk of them refugees from countries around the world that Edesia ships to, now legally living in Rhode Island. Navyn's the kind of boss who chats with everybody we run into in the factory and warehouse. When the warehouse manager spots us sitting on the floor and decides to bring over some chairs, she jokes--
<v Ira Glass>她完全没有制造经验，不得不学习一切，然后将业务发展到拥有150名员工的工厂，其中很大一部分员工是来自埃迪西亚发货的世界各地国家的难民，现在他们合法居住在罗得岛州。纳温是那种会和我们在工厂和仓库里遇到的每个人闲聊的老板。当仓库经理发现我们坐在地板上并决定搬来几把椅子时，她开玩笑说——

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:44.900
<v Navyn Salem>[LAUGHS] Do we look like we need help getting up?
<v Navyn Salem>[笑] 我们看起来需要人帮忙才能站起来吗？

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:46.650
<v Navyn Salem>[CHATTER]
<v Navyn Salem>[嘈杂声]

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:50.780
<v Navyn Salem>Yeah, yeah. You guys, we're fine. Don't worry.
<v Navyn Salem>对，对。伙计们，我们很好。别担心。

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:53.910
<v Ira Glass>Then she leans over to my producer, Ike, who's on the floor.
<v Ira Glass>然后她俯身对坐在地上的我的制片人艾克（Ike）说。

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:59.570
<v Navyn Salem>Ike, sit on the chair. Just pretend. Just, I didn't want to insult them.
<v Navyn Salem>艾克，坐在椅子上。假装一下。只是，我不想侮辱他们。

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:20.600
<v Ira Glass>Navyn describes her workday since January during this period of foreign aid limbo as an ongoing series of brand new, possibly business-ending, problems to solve, one after another. She got stop-work orders from the government for shipments that were already in production. Then the stop orders were reversed. This happened twice.
<v Ira Glass>纳温形容自1月以来在这段对外援助处于真空的时期里，她的工作日就是一连串全新的、可能导致公司倒闭的问题，一个接一个地需要解决。她收到了政府对已经在生产中的货物的停工令。然后停工令又被撤销了。这种情况发生了两次。

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:33.090
<v Ira Glass>In February, when almost all the 10,000 employees at USAID were let go, the contracting officer that Navyn dealt with was one of them. She suddenly found herself with nobody she could contact at the agency. Meanwhile--
<v Ira Glass>2月份，当美国国际开发署约1万名员工几乎全部被解雇时，与纳温对接的合同官就是其中之一。她突然发现自己在该机构里联系不到任何人。与此同时——

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:43.520
<v Navyn Salem>They broke the payment system. Imagine not knowing that your entire cash flow is going to be turned off with no warning, and try to run a business with no money.
<v Navyn Salem>他们破坏了支付系统。想象一下，在没有任何警告的情况下，你所有的现金流都将断裂，而你却要在没有资金的情况下尝试运营一家企业。

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:54.840
<v Ira Glass>The government owed her a bunch of payments, totaling $24 million for orders placed last year. When it didn't come, she had to shut down the production lines for the first time in 15 years.
<v Ira Glass>政府欠她一笔款项，总计2400万美元，用于去年的订单。当款项没有到账时，她15年来第一次不得不关闭生产线。

00:05:54.840 --> 00:06:20.740
<v Navyn Salem>We got the funniest messages, like, your invoices have been rejected. Thank you. [LAUGHS] Like, OK. The next obvious question is, why? Who is going to walk away and just be like, oh, that's fine? That was just $7 million. It's fine. You don't need to pay. Because you said they're rejected with no reason? OK. It's OK.
<v Navyn Salem>我们收到了最搞笑的信息，比如，您的发票已被拒绝。谢谢。[笑] 就像，好吧。下一个显而易见的问题是，为什么？谁会一走了之，然后说，哦，没关系？那可是700万美元。没关系。你不需要付钱。因为你没给出理由就说它们被拒绝了？好吧。没关系。

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:23.290
<v Ira Glass>And that's $7 million for stuff you've already made.
<v Ira Glass>那是你已经制造好的东西的700万美元。

00:06:23.290 --> 00:07:02.560
<v Navyn Salem>I have a binding contract with USAID. I bought the peanuts, I made the Plumpy'Nut, and I shipped it to the country that you required it to go to. And then you decided that you cannot pay me. And sometimes we get, "This contract has been canceled. Please resume your normal business activities." I'm like, you are my normal business activity. I can't resume if we can't get along here.
<v Navyn Salem>我与美国国际开发署有具有法律约束力的合同。我买了花生，我做了Plumpy'Nut，我把它运到了你要求去的国家。然后你决定你不能付钱给我。有时我们还会收到，“此合同已被取消。请恢复您的正常业务活动。”我说，你就是我的正常业务活动。如果我们在这里无法达成一致，我就无法恢复。

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:12.080
<v Ira Glass>The production line's conveyor belts are right outside her office, and she says she would sometimes look at them and think, every hour they were shut, that's 415 kids she couldn't feed.
<v Ira Glass>生产线的传送带就在她办公室外面，她说她有时会看着它们想，它们每停工一个小时，就有415个孩子吃不上饭。

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:22.240
<v Navyn Salem>That's the calculation that's going through my head. That's the urgency that I feel. And I'm trying to light things on fire to let people know.
<v Navyn Salem>这就是我脑海中盘算的计算结果。这就是我感受到的紧迫感。我在试图点燃一些东西，让人们知道。

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:47.770
<v Ira Glass>How she's handled this is a good illustration of what you have to do when normal systems to solve problems no longer exist. Navyn's basically been working the phones and texts and emails nonstop, talking to anybody who might have information or might know somebody that might help her stay in production. She's done a ton of press and says it's actually helped. It's led to random celebrities and people connected to the White House reaching out and trying to pull strings for her.
<v Ira Glass>她如何处理这件事很好地说明了当解决问题的常规系统不再存在时你必须做什么。纳温基本上一直在不停地打电话、发短信和发电子邮件，与任何可能掌握信息或可能认识能帮她保持生产的人交谈。她做了大量的媒体宣传，并表示这确实有所帮助。这促使一些名人和白宫相关人员主动联系并试图为她走后门。

00:07:47.770 --> 00:08:08.670
<v Ira Glass>Social media has helped, too. Once it became clear that the State Department was going to take over all the functions of USAID, Navyn posted on Instagram looking for the name of somebody, anybody inside the State Department who might help her get shipments going again. And, she says, incredibly, somebody saw that and reached out to say, oh, my god, I'm at dinner with the person you need.
<v Ira Glass>社交媒体也发挥了作用。一旦明显看出国务院将接管美国国际开发署的所有职能，纳温就在Instagram上发帖寻找国务院内部某个人的名字，无论是谁，只要能帮她重新开始发货。而且，她说，令人难以置信的是，有人看到了并联系她说，哦，天哪，我和你需要找的那个人在吃晚饭。

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:12.480
<v Ira Glass>And then is that person at the State Department, are they somebody who actually can help you?
<v Ira Glass>然后国务院的那个人，他是真正能帮到你的人吗？

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:15.140
<v Navyn Salem>He's the only person who can help me.
<v Navyn Salem>他是唯一能帮我的人。

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:16.800
<v Ira Glass>And how's that going?
<v Ira Glass>进展怎么样？

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:25.070
<v Navyn Salem>Well, imagine that you have a company, and you fired everybody, except the CEO.
<v Navyn Salem>好吧，想象一下你有一家公司，你解雇了所有人，只留下了首席执行官。

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:28.500
<v Ira Glass>This is basically the situation her contact at State Department is in.
<v Ira Glass>这基本上就是她在国务院的联系人所处的状况。

00:08:28.500 --> 00:09:10.740
<v Navyn Salem>Now, the CEO doesn't know how to do all the jobs in the company, so they can't. And even if all they want with all their heart's desire is to get a transportation contract, the person who writes them and drafts them is no longer there. So you can't. Then imagine that the CEO has to make every decision in the company, thousands of decisions a day, so-- And the CEO also just started less than two months ago. Yeah.
<v Navyn Salem>现在，首席执行官不知道如何做公司里的所有工作，所以他们做不到。而且，即使他们内心极其渴望获得一份运输合同，编写和起草合同的人也已经不在了。所以你做不到。然后想象一下，首席执行官必须做出公司里的每一个决定，一天数千个决定，所以——而且首席执行官也是不到两个月前刚上任的。是的。

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:31.710
<v Ira Glass>Step by step, some things have fallen in place. The government started paying again so she could restart her production lines in March. Her contracting officer came back, so she has somebody to talk to. It was last month when I sat on the warehouse floor with her and recorded this interview. And since then, about half of the boxes have shipped out. The rest are still stuck.
<v Ira Glass>一步一步地，一些事情落到了实处。政府开始重新付款，以便她在3月份重新启动生产线。她的合同官回来了，所以她有了可以交谈的人。上个月，我和她坐在仓库地板上录制了这个采访。从那以后，大约有一半的箱子已经运出。剩下的还在滞留。

00:09:31.710 --> 00:09:53.180
<v Ira Glass>But a bigger problem is the future. The Trump administration has said that life-saving aid will continue in some form for some countries. But the question is, how much? The State Department has only committed to one month's worth of RUTF production and nothing beyond it. And even that, they haven't issued contracts.
<v Ira Glass>但更大的问题是未来。特朗普政府表示，将以某种形式继续为一些国家提供拯救生命的援助。但问题是，多少？国务院仅承诺提供相当于一个月的RUTF产量，除此之外什么都没有。甚至连这些，他们也没有签发合同。

00:09:53.180 --> 00:10:05.240
<v Ira Glass>And there are people waiting for RUTF, all over the world. Dr. Mushtaq Khan oversees nutrition programs in Afghanistan for the International Rescue Committee and drops by their RUTF warehouse sometimes.
<v Ira Glass>全世界都有人在等待RUTF。穆什塔克·汗博士（Dr. Mushtaq Khan）在国际救援委员会负责阿富汗的营养项目，有时会顺道参观他们的RUTF仓库。

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:25.640
<v Mushtaq Khan>Now, that warehouse is kind of deserted. It should be the correct word. But yeah, I would say empty shelves. They are empty with empty pallets because the last bit of supply which we had, we shipped to our health facilities. So our central warehouse is empty now.
<v Mushtaq Khan>现在，那个仓库可以说是荒废了。这应该是个准确的词。但是，是的，我会说空空如也的货架。它们只有空托盘，因为我们将拥有的最后一批供应品运送到了我们的医疗设施。所以我们的中央仓库现在空了。

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:47.640
<v Ira Glass>Their health facilities around the country, the clinics that they shipped their RUTF to, have enough to last till the end of next month to the end of July. Dr. Khan says 900,000 children in Afghanistan fall into the category severely acute malnutrition, children so malnourished that babies don't have the energy to cry. And he's scared of how many will die if things don't change.
<v Ira Glass>他们在全国各地的医疗设施，即他们运送RUTF的诊所，库存仅够维持到下月底到7月底。汗博士说，阿富汗有90万儿童属于严重急性营养不良，这些儿童营养不良到婴儿都没有力气哭泣。他很担心如果情况没有改变，会有多少人死去。

00:10:47.640 --> 00:11:02.460
<v Mushtaq Khan>I'm really afraid of that, that it might end up with a catastrophic situation because these RUTFs are the lifeline for them. Without those, this might be the worst case scenario.
<v Mushtaq Khan>我真的很担心这一点，它可能会以灾难性的情况告终，因为这些RUTF是他们的生命线。如果没有这些，这可能是最糟糕的情况。

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:30.240
<v Ira Glass>And it might be. I didn't know this when we spoke, but the Trump administration has said that it is not going to be sending any aid of any kind to Afghanistan, where Dr. Khan is, because, it says, that aid benefited terrorist groups. This came after the administration suspended the people that USAID had in place to make sure that it did not go to terrorist groups.
<v Ira Glass>确实可能如此。我们交谈时我还不知道这一点，但特朗普政府表示，它不会向汗博士所在的阿富汗提供任何形式的援助，因为它称这些援助使恐怖组织受益。在此之前，该政府暂停了美国国际开发署派驻的确保援助不会落入恐怖组织手中的人员的职务。

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:56.080
<v Ira Glass>July 1, just a couple of weeks from now, the State Department is supposed to finish taking over all the things that USAID used to do, whatever things are left. And as this period of limbo ends, this period of transition between agencies, as we all wait to see what scraps of American foreign aid remain when it's done, we thought it would be a good moment to ask, what was that all about, USAID?
<v Ira Glass>7月1日，也就是从现在算起的短短几周后，国务院应该完成接管美国国际开发署过去所做的一切事情，无论还剩下什么事情。当这段不确定时期结束时，在这个机构之间的过渡期，当我们都在等待看美国对外援助在结束后还剩下什么残羹剩饭时，我们认为这是一个好时机来问问，美国国际开发署到底是怎么回事？

00:11:56.080 --> 00:12:42.310
<v Ira Glass>The recent public conversation about this tends to be either outraged Democrats defending our decades of foreign aid or conservatives of all kinds pointing out waste and corruption. The truth is even diehard, lifelong advocates of foreign aid said it wasn't perfect. We thought we'd take some time here today to step back from the regular pro and con positions on this one and ask, what were the good things that aid accomplished? What projects did more harm than good? How should we think about this, now that so much of it seems to be going away? Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>最近关于这一话题的公开讨论往往要么是愤怒的民主党人捍卫我们几十年的对外援助，要么是各种保守派指出浪费和腐败。事实是，即使是坚定的、终身的对外援助倡导者也承认它并非完美无缺。今天我们想花点时间在这里从关于这一点的常规赞成和反对立场中抽身出来，问问：援助取得的好成绩是什么？哪些项目危害大于益处？既然其中很大一部分似乎正在消失，我们该如何看待这件事？请继续收听。

00:12:42.310 --> 00:13:02.010
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. Act One, 63 Years, 7 Months, 26 Days. That length of time, that is the entire existence of the federal agency USAID, from the day it was created in November of 1961, until the last day of this month, which the Trump administration hopes will be the last day it is running any operations.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。第一幕，63年7个月26天。这段时间，就是联邦机构美国国际开发署的整个存在时间，从它于1961年11月成立的那天起，直到本月底的最后一天，特朗普政府希望那将是它运营任何业务的最后一天。

00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:20.000
<v Ira Glass>During those years, the agency spent hundreds of billions of dollars. The United States was the largest humanitarian donor in the world. So what did it buy? Where was that money well spent, and where was it a waste? David Kestenbaum on our staff talked with two people who know that history well.
<v Ira Glass>在这些年里，该机构花费了数千亿美元。美国是世界上最大的国际人道主义捐助国。那么这些钱都买到了什么？哪些钱花得物有所值，哪些又是浪费？我们团队的大卫·克斯滕鲍姆（David Kestenbaum）与两位对这段历史非常了解的人进行了交谈。

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:41.900
<v David Kestenbaum>The year USAID was founded, John F. Kennedy was president, and he was trying to figure out what the US should be in the world. It was an interesting moment for that. The year before, 17 countries in Africa had gained independence from decades of colonial rule. These were new nations. India had also become independent some years before, and Indonesia, other countries, too.
<v David Kestenbaum>美国国际开发署成立的那一年，约翰·F·肯尼迪（John F. Kennedy）担任总统，他当时正在努力弄清楚美国在世界上应该扮演什么角色。对于这一点来说，这是一个有趣的时刻。前一年，非洲有17个国家从几十年的殖民统治中独立出来。这些都是新兴国家。印度也在几年前独立，印度尼西亚以及其他国家也是如此。

00:13:41.900 --> 00:13:50.910
<v David Kestenbaum>Joshua Craze wrote about all this recently in The New York Review of Books, really, trying to do what we're trying to do in today's show-- weigh up the good and the bad of what followed.
<v David Kestenbaum>约书亚·克雷兹（Joshua Craze）最近在《纽约书评》上撰文谈到了这一切，实际上，他试图做我们今天节目中试图做的事情——权衡随之而来的好坏。

00:13:50.910 --> 00:14:06.330
<v Joshua Craze>I think for Kennedy, the goal is to bring development to the rest of the world. That's the stated goal. There are new nations around the world. And he says, look, what we're doing is going to bring up these nations, just like we brought up Europe after World War II.
<v Joshua Craze>我认为对于肯尼迪来说，目标是将发展带到世界其他地方。这是声明的目标。世界各地都有新兴国家。他说，看，我们正在做的事情将提升这些国家，就像我们在二战后提升欧洲一样。

00:14:06.330 --> 00:14:31.230
<v David Kestenbaum>After World War II, with the Marshall Plan, we gave massive assistance to European countries that had been wrecked by the war. And it was a huge success. Their economies rebounded. But Kennedy didn't want to help these new countries just because it seemed like the right thing to do. There was another reason, kind of big in 1961-- he didn't want them to become Communist. The US was worried all these countries were going to fall under Soviet influence.
<v David Kestenbaum>二战后，通过马歇尔计划，我们向遭到战争破坏的欧洲国家提供了大量援助。这是一个巨大的成功。他们的经济复苏了。但肯尼迪帮助这些新兴国家不仅仅是因为这看起来是正确的事情。还有一个原因，在1961年相当重大——他不希望他们变成共产主义者。美国担心所有这些国家都会陷入苏联的影响力。

00:14:31.230 --> 00:14:43.220
<v Joshua Craze>When he first starts USAID, Kennedy addresses all the heads of the agency and says to them, in many places where freedom is under threat, we will not send Marines. We will send you.
<v Joshua Craze>肯尼迪在刚开始成立美国国际开发署时，向该机构的所有负责人发表讲话，对他们说，在许多自由受到威胁的地方，我们不会派遣海军陆战队。我们会派你们去。

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:52.640
<v Joshua Craze>(SUBJECT) JOHN F. KENNEDY: We send you. And you working with the people in those countries to try to work with them in developing the--
<v Joshua Craze>（主题）约翰·F·肯尼迪：我们派你们去。你们与那些国家的人民合作，试图与他们合作开发——

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:54.520
<v David Kestenbaum>So what are those first projects like?
<v David Kestenbaum>那么，最初的这些项目是什么样的？

00:14:54.520 --> 00:15:12.710
<v Joshua Craze>You have huge variety of projects. You have massive agricultural schemes to modernize agriculture in Southeast Asia. You have schemes to create banking systems inside Africa. You have schemes to create democracy and education. You have free press.
<v Joshua Craze>你会看到各种各样的项目。比如在东南亚推行农业现代化的庞大农业计划，在非洲内部建立银行业系统的计划，建立民主和教育的计划，还有自由新闻。

00:15:12.710 --> 00:15:28.210
<v Joshua Craze>You have all sorts of-- like, take America, look around the world, and go, is this thing there? And if America's not there, we just try to make it like America. That was the goal, you know? And there were a lot of very idealistic people doing this work.
<v Joshua Craze>可谓应有尽有——比如，以美国为例，环顾世界，然后去想，这个地方有这东西吗？如果美国不在那里，我们就试着把它变得像美国一样。这就是目标，你知道的？而且有很多人怀着非常理想主义的心情在做这项工作。

00:15:28.210 --> 00:15:49.140
<v David Kestenbaum>Huge, ambitious, and maybe ill-conceived goal-- make every place more like America. How well did it work? In most cases, not very well. I talked to John Norris about this. He wrote what, as far as I can tell, is the only really straight-up history of USAID, a book called The Enduring Struggle. He also worked at USAID for a while.
<v David Kestenbaum>这是一个巨大、雄心勃勃、或许并不成熟的目标——让每一个地方都更像美国。效果如何？在大多数情况下，效果并不好。我为此采访了约翰·诺里斯。据我所知，他写了唯一一本真正直面美国国际开发署历史的书，书名叫《持久的斗争》（The Enduring Struggle）。他也曾在美国国际开发署工作过一段时间。

00:15:49.140 --> 00:16:03.660
<v David Kestenbaum>Norris says for development to work, the country's government has to make a bunch of difficult changes-- enact economic reforms, invest in education. You need legal systems and contracts, all sorts of stuff. And you need a government with the will to push all that through.
<v David Kestenbaum>诺里斯表示，要想让发展发挥作用，一个国家的政府必须做出许多艰难的改变——实施经济改革，投资教育。你需要法律体系和契约，以及各种各样的事物。你还需要一个有决心将这一切推行到底的政府。

00:16:03.660 --> 00:16:17.550
<v David Kestenbaum>Some countries were run by autocrats who didn't really care for helping the general population. And the ones who did care? It was still hard to make those changes. It would be for any nation. And, John Norris says, colonialism had really damaged a lot of countries.
<v David Kestenbaum>有些国家由独裁者统治，他们并不真正关心如何帮助普通民众。而那些真正关心的呢？要做出这些改变依然很难。对任何国家来说都是如此。而且，约翰·诺里斯指出，殖民主义确实对许多国家造成了极大的破坏。

00:16:17.550 --> 00:16:41.060
<v John Norris>They had always been actively discouraged from developing a civil service or engineers or doctors by their colonial leaders. And literally, countries emerging in independence, where there might only be a handful of doctors, lawyers, or engineers that have been trained in the country, very little in the way of competent civil service, that is a very low base to start from.
<v John Norris>它们的殖民统治者历来积极劝阻它们发展文官制度、工程师队伍或医生队伍。真的，那些刚刚获得独立的国家，国内可能只有区区几名受过本土培训的医生、律师或工程师，几乎没有什么称职的文官队伍，这真是一个起点极低的烂摊子。

00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:43.550
<v David Kestenbaum>There are a couple real successes, though.
<v David Kestenbaum>不过，也有少数真正成功的例子。

00:16:43.550 --> 00:17:13.510
<v John Norris>The textbook example is, really, South Korea. South Korea, in the early '60s, was absolutely devastated. The country had labored under a brutal Japanese occupation for decades. It had been devastated by the Korean War. The countryside was pretty much in ruin. And then a really remarkable set of changes began to happen, with a lot help from US assistance.
<v John Norris>教科书级别的典范其实是韩国。20世纪60年代初的韩国简直满目疮痍。该国遭受了几十年残酷的日本殖民统治的折磨，又在朝鲜战争中遭到毁灭性破坏。农村地区几乎沦为废墟。随后，在美方的大力援助下，一系列非常显著的变化开始发生。

00:17:13.510 --> 00:17:42.090
<v David Kestenbaum>All kinds of economic reforms. The government distributed land to small farmers, invested in schools. The US trained government officials on budgeting and on economic policy. A major reason it worked was the South Korean president, Park Chung Hee, who forced through all kinds of economic changes. One historian said he instituted a, quote, "developmental dictatorship." Today, South Korea is one of the largest economies in the world.
<v David Kestenbaum>各种经济改革。政府向小农分发土地，投资学校。美国培训了政府官员的预算编制和经济政策能力。它之所以能取得巨大成功，主要归功于韩国总统朴正熙，他强行推行了各种经济变革。一位历史学家称他建立了一个“发展型独裁”。如今，韩国已跻身全球最大经济体之列。

00:17:42.090 --> 00:17:45.960
<v David Kestenbaum>How involved was USAID in all those changes in South Korea?
<v David Kestenbaum>美国国际开发署在韩国的这些变革中参与度有多高？

00:17:45.960 --> 00:18:12.960
<v John Norris>Incredibly, that they would have a weekly meeting with senior cabinet officials on economic reform, on education, on agrarian policy. South Korean officials were flown to the United States to meet with business leaders to understand how they could better prepare their exports for the US market. It was day to day, week to week, and very intense.
<v John Norris>极其深厚，他们会与高级内阁官员举行每周例会，讨论经济改革、教育和农业政策。韩国官员被空运到美国与商界领袖会面，以了解如何更好地为美国市场准备出口产品。这是日复一日、周复一周的，而且非常密集。

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:24.000
<v David Kestenbaum>So there is South Korea. Taiwan is another example. But overall, pretty mixed bag. John Norris says there is one more category of things that did not work that's worth including here.
<v David Kestenbaum>所以，这就是韩国。台湾是另一个例子。但总体而言，这是一个毁誉参半的杂烩。约翰·诺里斯表示，还有另一类没有成功、但也值得在此提及的事物。

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:40.590
<v John Norris>There's a consistent theme to the places where AID had its biggest failures, and it's in those places where it was used as a blunt instrument of US foreign and security policy.
<v John Norris>美国国际开发署遭遇最大挫折的地方有一个共同的主题，那就是在这些地方，它被当作了美国外交和安全政策的生硬工具。

00:18:40.590 --> 00:19:08.550
<v David Kestenbaum>The wars in Afghanistan, Iraq, and, before that, Vietnam, the US spent billions and billions of dollars in aid money, he says, basically, to try to win people over and prop up the governments that were our allies, which is not a recipe for success. At one point, a quarter of all USAID global staff was operating out of Vietnam, without much to show for it.
<v David Kestenbaum>他说，在阿富汗、伊拉克以及之前的越南战争中，美国花费了数以百亿计的援助资金，基本上是为了试图赢得人心并扶持那些作为我们盟友的政府，这绝不是通往成功的秘诀。曾几何时，美国国际开发署全球员工中有四分之一在越南开展工作，却并没有取得多大成效。

00:19:08.550 --> 00:19:18.220
<v David Kestenbaum>So that was the first goal-- help countries develop economically. There was a second goal for USAID, though-- to fight communism. How did that go?
<v David Kestenbaum>这就是第一个目标——帮助各国实现经济发展。不过，美国国际开发署还有第二个目标——反共。进展如何？

00:19:18.220 --> 00:19:25.740
<v Joshua Craze>Well, communism is gone, except for like eight friends of mine in Oakland. So I think it's--
<v Joshua Craze>嗯，共产主义已经消失了，除了我在奥克兰的那大概八个老朋友。所以我觉得——

00:19:25.740 --> 00:19:26.240
<v Joshua Craze>[LAUGHTER]
<v Joshua Craze>[笑声]

00:19:26.070 --> 00:19:26.980
<v David Kestenbaum>Thanks to USAID.
<v David Kestenbaum>多亏了美国国际开发署。

00:19:26.980 --> 00:19:28.360
<v Joshua Craze>Yes, thanks to USAID.
<v Joshua Craze>是的，多亏了美国国际开发署。

00:19:28.360 --> 00:19:31.760
<v David Kestenbaum>Neither of them saw USAID playing a big role.
<v David Kestenbaum>两人都不认为美国国际开发署发挥了多么巨大的作用。

00:19:31.760 --> 00:19:43.090
<v John Norris>I would give them a solid star for the effort. Certainly, the Soviets collapsed because it was a miserable system that was always designed to fail.
<v John Norris>我会给他们的努力打个扎实的满分。当然，苏联的崩溃是因为它本身就是一个注定要失败的可悲系统。

00:19:43.090 --> 00:20:05.530
<v David Kestenbaum>And, very broad strokes, that is the first half of USAID's history. There's a real change in the second half. Communism is gone now, so that can't be the mission anymore. Helping countries develop seems hard. So there is this shift to something much more basic-- trying to assist people most in need, humanitarian aid, and fighting disease.
<v David Kestenbaum>抛开细节不谈，这就是美国国际开发署历史的前半部分。后半部分发生了真正的转变。共产主义现在已经消失了，所以这不再可能是使命。帮助各国发展看起来很艰难。于是，重心转向了更基础的事情——试图援助最需要帮助的人、提供人道主义援助以及抗击疾病。

00:20:05.530 --> 00:20:25.400
<v David Kestenbaum>In fact, when USAID was shut down this year, these were the largest parts of its spending. In 2024, out of $35 billion of programs it managed, about $10 billion was going to humanitarian aid and $10 billion for health. There is good and less good here, also, over the years. First, the good-- we eradicated smallpox.
<v David Kestenbaum>事实上，当美国国际开发署今年关闭时，这些正是其支出的最大组成部分。在2024年它管理的350亿美元项目中，大约100亿美元用于人道主义援助，100亿美元用于医疗健康。多年来，这里面既有好的一面，也有不那么好的一面。首先是好的一面——我们消灭了天花。

00:20:25.400 --> 00:20:49.910
<v John Norris>Smallpox claimed 300 million lives in the 20th century alone, 300 million. That is just an eye-popping number. A joint effort by USAID, CDC, the World Health Organization, and the Soviet Union, and some innovative technology wiped smallpox off the map, the first time in human history that a disease had been eradicated.
<v John Norris>仅在20世纪，天花就夺去了3亿人的生命，整整3亿人。这是一个令人瞠目结舌的数字。美国国际开发署、美国疾病控制与预防中心、世界卫生组织以及苏联共同努力，加上一些创新技术，将天花从地图上彻底抹去，这是人类历史上第一次彻底消灭一种疾病。

00:20:49.910 --> 00:21:17.180
<v John Norris>And the benefits of it-- and this is one of the things where people forget how powerful the returns are of assistance for the United States itself-- the entire amount that we spent on smallpox, in the effort to eradicate it, and delivering all those vaccines around the globe, we save that same amount every 24 days, 24 days, because we don't have to vaccinate Americans against smallpox.
<v John Norris>而它的好处——这也是人们经常忘记的一点，即援助对美国自身的回报有多么强大——我们在消灭天花、向全球运送所有这些疫苗的努力中所花费的全部资金，每隔24天、仅仅24天，我们就能通过不再需要为美国人接种天花疫苗而省下同等数额的钱。

00:21:17.180 --> 00:21:33.480
<v David Kestenbaum>Actually, it's 26 days, but still a win. Not every example is so clean, though. Joshua Craze says the aid sometimes props up governments that aren't so great. Even sending in food can be complicated. He spent a lot of time covering the conflicts in Sudan and South Sudan.
<v David Kestenbaum>实际上是26天，但依然是一场胜利。不过，并非每个例子都这么纯粹。约书亚·科雷兹（Joshua Craze）表示，这些援助有时会支撑起那些并非那么优秀的政府。甚至连运送粮食也可能变得很复杂。他花了很多时间报道苏丹和南苏丹的冲突。

00:21:33.480 --> 00:21:47.560
<v Joshua Craze>Very often, that humanitarian aid is diverted to the government or to the very people conducting campaigns of ethnic cleansing. Then there's the question of, OK, how do you get the food aid to people? Well, you have to hire trucking companies. Who owns the trucking companies?
<v Joshua Craze>很多时候，人道主义援助被转移给了政府，或者直接落入了正在进行种族清洗活动的人手中。接着就是这个问题：好吧，你如何将粮食援助送到人们手中？好吧，你必须雇用卡车公司。谁拥有这些卡车公司？

00:21:47.560 --> 00:21:50.320
<v David Kestenbaum>Sometimes the trucking companies are connected to the government.
<v David Kestenbaum>有时卡车公司与政府有着千丝万缕的联系。

00:21:50.320 --> 00:22:15.600
<v Joshua Craze>And so you have this most disgusting logic sometimes, which is that the humanitarians end up sustaining the financial base of the very people who've displaced the civilians that the humanitarians tend to. So they support the hell that they're supposed to minister.
<v Joshua Craze>因此，有时你会遇到这种极其荒谬的逻辑，即人道主义者最终维持了那些驱逐了人道主义者所照顾平民的人的财政基础。所以他们是在支持他们本该去治理的地狱。

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:20.220
<v David Kestenbaum>What do you make of the Trump administration's critique of USAID?
<v David Kestenbaum>你如何看待特朗普政府对美国国际开发署的批评？

00:22:20.220 --> 00:22:46.480
<v Joshua Craze>So different parts of the Trump administration had different critiques of USAID. One critique was USAID is an organization that pushes a particular political agenda around the world that is not very American. I think that was a distraction from what they were actually doing. You could have cut all of the supposed transgender operas in Bolivia and left the core mission.
<v Joshua Craze>特朗普政府的不同部门对美国国际开发署有着不同的批评。其中一个批评是，美国国际开发署是一个在世界各地推行某种特定政治议程的组织，而这并不太符合美国利益。我认为这转移了人们对其实际工作重心的注意力。你完全可以砍掉所有波利维亚那些所谓跨性别歌剧，而保留核心使命。

00:22:46.480 --> 00:23:12.160
<v David Kestenbaum>That is one explanation the Trump administration has offered. It put out a press release listing a handful of projects it considered waste, fraud, and abuse, including a DEI musical in Ireland, which seems like it was from 2022, also an electric vehicle program in Vietnam. Most were pretty small ticket items. Secretary of State Marco Rubio has offered a different critique, that USAID was funding things that were not in the US national interest.
<v David Kestenbaum>这是特朗普政府给出的解释之一。它发布了一份新闻稿，列出了一小撮被认为是浪费、欺诈和滥用的项目，其中包括爱尔兰的一个多元化、平等与包容（DEI）音乐剧（似乎是2022年的），以及越南的一个电动汽车项目。大多数都是金额相当小的项目。国务卿马可·鲁比奥提出了另一种批评，即美国国际开发署资助的项目并不符合美国的国家利益。

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:25.040
<v Joshua Craze>The other critique of USAID is bound up in this bigger issue for the Trump administration, which is, why is the US paying for everything? We are paying-- we've got to get our-- I'm sorry for my American accent-- but like, we've gotta get our money back somehow.
<v Joshua Craze>对美国国际开发署的另一项批评与特朗普政府的这个更大议题息息相关，那就是：凭什么美国要为一切买单？我们在付钱——我们必须把我们的——抱歉我的美国口音——但是，就像我们需要设法把钱拿回来一样。

00:23:25.040 --> 00:23:52.210
<v Joshua Craze>And this is a really, for me, simplistic view of politics and economics. Dollar dominance has been so good for America. And dollar dominance is carried out because it is the world currency. And that's also guaranteed by things like USAID. You know, that the Trump administration is worried that you're not getting respect from South Africa because-- but you're giving malaria drugs.
<v Joshua Craze>在我看来，这是一种非常简单化的政治和经济观点。美元霸权对美国太有利了。而美元霸权得以维系，是因为它是世界货币。这也是由美国国际开发署之类的事物所担保的。你知道的，特朗普政府担心你没有从南非那里获得尊重，因为——但你却在分发抗疟疾药物。

00:23:52.210 --> 00:24:21.900
<v Joshua Craze>The reality is, until three months ago, everywhere I went in the Horn of Africa, people thought the one space of goodness and hope in the world was America. And they might have been wrong about that in many ways. And America might have done all sorts of bad things and been imperialist in all sorts of ways. But it was a real belief, and it's gone. And I just-- I think that's, from the perspective of American hegemony, an incalculable loss.
<v Joshua Craze>现实情况是，直到三个月前，我在非洲之角去过的任何地方，人们都认为世界上唯一充满善良和希望的地方就是美国。在许多方面，他们对这一点可能看法有误。而且美国可能做过各种坏事，并在各种方面表现出帝国主义行径。但这是一种真实的信念，而它现在消失了。我只是——我认为，从美国霸权的角度来看，这是一场无法估量的损失。

00:24:21.900 --> 00:24:41.800
<v David Kestenbaum>John Norris had similar feelings, despite all the things over the years that hadn't worked. When some future historian goes to write this next chapter, he worries, it's not going to be a good one.
<v David Kestenbaum>约翰·诺里斯也有类似的情绪，尽管多年来有那么多项目最终流产。他担心，当未来的某位历史学家去书写这下一个章节时，这不会是一个美好的结局。

00:24:41.800 --> 00:24:59.960
<v Ira Glass>David Kestenbaum, the senior editor of our show. Coming up, we go visit a hospital funded by USAID. It's pretty nice. Too nice? That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·克斯滕鲍姆（David Kestenbaum），我们节目的资深编辑。接下来，我们将去参观一家由美国国际开发署资助的医院。它相当不错。好过头了吗？节目稍后继续，由芝加哥公共广播电台为您播出。

00:24:59.960 --> 00:25:17.200
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program. Some Things We Don't Do Anymore, stories of this period that we're going through right now, where the Trump administration stopped everything USAID was funding, then pledged to restart a lot of it, especially life-saving assistance, food, and medicines.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《此生美国》（This American Life）。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。今天的节目。有些事我们不再做了（Some Things We Don't Do Anymore），讲述我们目前正在经历的这一时期的故事：特朗普政府叫停了美国国际开发署资助的一切项目，随后承诺重启其中的许多项目，尤其是拯救生命的援助、食品和药品。

00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:33.410
<v Ira Glass>Right now when you add it up, over half the money USAID spends is supposed to continue. But most of that money has not gone out, and it's unclear how much of it ever will. We've arrived at Act Two of our program. Act Two, Case Study.
<v Ira Glass>现在加起来，美国国际开发署支出的资金中，有一半以上预计将继续发放。但其中大部分资金尚未拨付，而且不清楚其中究竟有多少会真正到位。我们来到了节目的第二幕。第二幕，案例研究。

00:25:33.410 --> 00:25:52.010
<v Ira Glass>OK, so what happens when you pump hundreds of millions of US dollars into a country? We're interested in both the good things that happen and the bad. And the place that we picked to look at this is a tiny African country called Eswatini, only 1.2 million people, right between South Africa and Mozambique. It used to be called Swaziland.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，当你向一个国家注入数亿美元时会发生什么？我们既对发生的好事感兴趣，也对坏事感兴趣。我们选择用来观察这个问题的地点是一个名为斯威士兰（现称埃斯瓦蒂尼）的小型非洲国家，人口仅120万，正好夹在南非和莫桑比克之间。它以前被称为斯威士兰。

00:25:52.010 --> 00:26:17.780
<v Ira Glass>They're interesting for a few reasons. One is that they were part of one of the most successful things USAID has ever done, a program called PEPFAR, billions of dollars in over 50 countries that everybody agrees saved millions of lives. PEPFAR was created to combat AIDS. And for a long time, the country with the highest percentage of people with HIV has been Eswatini. So Eswatini is a good place to measure whether the aid has helped.
<v Ira Glass>它们之所以引人关注，原因有几个。其中之一是它们参与了美国国际开发署有史以来最成功的项目之一，即所谓的PEPFAR（总统艾滋病紧急救援计划），该计划在50多个国家投入了数十亿美元，所有人都一致认为它拯救了数百万人的生命。PEPFAR旨在抗击艾滋病。长期以来，HIV感染率最高的国家一直都是埃斯瓦蒂尼。因此，埃斯瓦蒂尼是衡量援助是否发挥作用的一个好地方。

00:26:17.780 --> 00:26:37.860
<v Ira Glass>But Eswatini also seemed like a good place to look at for another reason. And this reason we heard about from a Washington Post reporter named Chico Harlan. Chico had been calling around to all kinds of organizations that had lost their funding when the Trump administration froze all that in January. Most of them did not want to talk on the record. They thought it might hurt their chances of getting funding in the future.
<v Ira Glass>但埃斯瓦蒂尼似乎也是另一个值得观察的好地方。我们是从《华盛顿邮报》记者奇科·哈兰（Chico Harlan）那里听到这个原因的。奇科曾致电各种各样的组织，由于特朗普政府在1月冻结了所有资金，这些组织都失去了资助。他们大多数人都不愿意公开接受采访。他们认为这可能会损害他们未来获得资助的机会。

00:26:37.860 --> 00:27:05.280
<v Ira Glass>But Chico found a health provider in Eswatini called the Luke Commission that got a ton of USAID money in the past, whose leaders were not only willing to talk, they were pretty frank in expressing their own doubts and questions about whether the money they had gotten was entirely a good thing for Eswatini. They were grappling with the good and bad of it. So, for instance, here's one of the founders of the Luke Commission, Harry VanderWal, an American doctor who's been in Eswatini for 20 years, talking about the Trump funding cuts.
<v Ira Glass>但奇科在埃斯瓦蒂尼找到了一家名为卢克委员会（the Luke Commission）的医疗服务机构，该机构过去曾获得过美国国际开发署的大笔资金。其领导人不仅愿意交谈，而且在表达自己的疑虑和问题时相当坦诚，他们质疑他们得到的资金对埃斯瓦蒂尼来说是否完全是一件好事。他们正在努力权衡其中的利弊。例如，这是卢克委员会的创始人之一哈里·范德瓦尔（Harry VanderWal），一位在埃斯瓦蒂尼生活了20年的美国医生，他在谈论特朗普的资金削减时这样说道。

00:27:05.280 --> 00:27:39.500
<v Harry VanderWal>Actually, I understand why they're doing what they're doing. I get it, and I'm not against it at all. I see that-- I can see that as a business strategy that makes sense. Is it painful for those on the other side of it? Of course. And but as long as we do what we can to make sure people remain on medication-- and is that the responsibility of the US government to make sure people stay on medication? I don't know if I can say that fully. This has been going on for decades now. It's not necessarily their-- they can't take care of the whole world and their medical needs.
<v Harry VanderWal>实际上，我理解他们为什么要这么做。我懂，而且我一点也不反对。我看到——我可以将之视为一种讲得通的商业策略。对于身处其另一端的人来说这很痛苦吗？当然。但是，只要我们尽我们所能确保人们继续服药——而确保人们继续服药是美国政府的责任吗？我不知道自己能否完全这样说。这种情况现在已经持续了几十年。这不一定是他们的——他们无法照顾全世界及其医疗需求。

00:27:39.500 --> 00:27:54.940
<v Ira Glass>So Chico and one of our producers, Diane Wu, went to Eswatini to see, what exactly did American taxpayer money accomplish there? And what went wrong? They also get into it more with Harry later in the story. Here they are.
<v Ira Glass>于是，奇科和我们的制作人黛安·吴（Diane Wu）前往埃斯瓦蒂尼，去看看美国纳税人的钱到底在那里取得了什么成就？又有什么地方出了问题？在故事的后面，他们还会与哈里进行更多探讨。他们来了。

00:27:54.940 --> 00:27:59.390
<v Chico Harlan>The first thing you see when you turn into the Luke Commission's campus these days is a gate.
<v Chico Harlan>如今，当你拐进卢克委员会的园区时，映入眼帘的第一样东西是一扇大门。

00:27:59.390 --> 00:28:01.730
<v Diane Wu>There's the USAID and PEPFAR logos.
<v Diane Wu>上面有美国国际开发署和PEPFAR的标志。

00:28:01.730 --> 00:28:04.460
<v Chico Harlan>And there's a sign that says "Closed until further notice."
<v Chico Harlan>还有一个牌子写着“暂停营业，听候通知”。

00:28:04.460 --> 00:28:07.100
<v Ira Glass>We pull up and get checked in by a security guard--
<v Ira Glass>我们将车停下，由一名保安进行安检——

00:28:07.100 --> 00:28:07.600
<v Chico Harlan>Hi.
<v Chico Harlan>你好。

00:28:07.540 --> 00:28:08.040
<v Guard>How are you?
<v Guard>你好吗？

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:08.660
<v Chico Harlan>Good morning.
<v Chico Harlan>早上好。

00:28:08.660 --> 00:28:12.740
<v Ira Glass>--who then runs through the manicured grounds ahead of our car to show us where to park.
<v Ira Glass>——随后，他跑过车前修剪整齐的草坪，给我们带路去停车。

00:28:12.740 --> 00:28:15.170
<v Diane Wu>I feel like we're pulling up to a luxury hotel.
<v Diane Wu>我觉得我们像是开到了某个豪华酒店。

00:28:15.170 --> 00:28:20.870
<v Chico Harlan>Yeah, look, the rocks, the landscaping, the very moody lighting.
<v Chico Harlan>对，你看，这些石头、景观设计，还有非常情绪化的灯光。

00:28:20.870 --> 00:28:27.210
<v Ira Glass>There are these modern-looking sconces lighting the entrance to a slight gray building we pull up in front of, string lights.
<v Ira Glass>在我们停下的那一栋略显灰暗的建筑物入口处，挂着这些现代风格的壁灯和串灯。

00:28:27.210 --> 00:28:33.060
<v Diane Wu>Harry and Echo, the founders of the Luke Commission, are standing in front of the building to greet us.
<v Diane Wu>卢克委员会的创始哈里和埃科正站在大楼前迎接我们。

00:28:33.060 --> 00:28:34.200
<v Chico Harlan>Hi, Echo.
<v Chico Harlan>嗨，埃科。

00:28:34.200 --> 00:28:34.770
<v Echo VanderWal>Good to meet you.
<v Echo VanderWal>很高兴认识你。

00:28:34.770 --> 00:28:36.090
<v Chico Harlan>Likewise, at last.
<v Chico Harlan>彼此彼此，终于见面了。

00:28:36.090 --> 00:28:36.590
<v Echo VanderWal>Thanks.
<v Echo VanderWal>谢谢。

00:28:36.390 --> 00:28:36.890
<v Harry VanderWal>I'm Harry.
<v Harry VanderWal>我是哈里。

00:28:36.630 --> 00:28:37.520
<v Chico Harlan>Harry, nice to meet you.
<v Chico Harlan>哈里，很高兴认识你。

00:28:37.520 --> 00:28:54.870
<v Diane Wu>Echo and Harry met in college and married right after. They both had this Christian calling to do medical work overseas. So Harry became a doctor, and Echo became a physician's assistant. As Harry was finishing up his residency, they heard about Eswatini from a friend and thought, that seems like a good place to go and help.
<v Diane Wu>埃科和哈里在大学相识，毕业后不久就结婚了。两人都有去海外从事医疗工作的基督教使命感。于是哈里成了一名医生，埃科成了一名医师助理。正当哈里即将完成住院医师实习期时，他们从一个朋友那里听说了埃斯瓦蒂尼，觉得那似乎是一个去那里施以援手的好地方。

00:28:54.870 --> 00:28:56.570
<v Chico Harlan>Had either of you been to Africa before?
<v Chico Harlan>你们俩以前去过非洲吗？

00:28:56.570 --> 00:28:57.070
<v Echo VanderWal>Never.
<v Echo VanderWal>从来没有。

00:28:57.050 --> 00:28:57.630
<v Harry VanderWal>No.
<v Harry VanderWal>没有。

00:28:57.630 --> 00:29:00.000
<v Echo VanderWal>I didn't even have a passport before. Had you ever been out of the country?
<v Echo VanderWal>我以前连护照都没有。你以前出过国吗？

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:02.580
<v Harry VanderWal>Just to Canada, but didn't need a passport at that point, I don't think.
<v Harry VanderWal>只去过加拿大，但我觉得当时不需要护照。

00:29:02.580 --> 00:29:03.140
<v Echo VanderWal>Yeah.
<v Echo VanderWal>对。

00:29:03.140 --> 00:29:29.730
<v Chico Harlan>Right after Harry graduated, they packed up and moved, the two of them, plus three-year-old triplets and an infant. And if that sounds kind of bold, let me say, that is Echo. She is the CEO boss type, always with a plan and some talking points, calculatedly daring. I think that's one of the reasons she's the rare aid recipient willing to talk to journalists. She's betting it might help. She's the public face of the Luke Commission.
<v Chico Harlan>在哈里毕业后不久，他们收拾行李搬了家，夫妻俩加上三个三岁的多胞胎和一个婴儿。如果这听起来有点大胆，我得说，这就是埃科。她是那种首席执行官老板型的人物，总是带着计划和谈话要点，经过深思熟虑的勇敢。我认为这是她成为极少数愿意与记者交谈的援助接受者原因之一。她在赌这可能会有帮助。她是卢克委员会的公众形象。

00:29:29.730 --> 00:29:35.760
<v Echo VanderWal>When we first had the kids, he said, if you do the emails, the phone calls, and the meetings, I'll take care of the family.
<v Echo VanderWal>当我们刚有孩子时，他说，如果你负责发邮件、打电话和开会，我来照顾家庭。

00:29:35.760 --> 00:29:39.880
<v Harry VanderWal>I'm happy to let her talk. I don't always communicate very well, so it's better if she does the speaking.
<v Harry VanderWal>我很乐意让她去说。我表达能力有时不太好，所以由她来发言会更好。

00:29:39.880 --> 00:29:41.750
<v Harry VanderWal>[LAUGHTER]
<v Harry VanderWal>[笑声]

00:29:41.750 --> 00:29:51.170
<v Echo VanderWal>He's-- he's a doctor in internal medicine and pediatrics, so he's the smart one. I guess, I'm the practical one, maybe if we put it that way. Yeah.
<v Echo VanderWal>他——他是一名内科和儿科医生，所以他是聪明的那一个。我想，我是务实的那一个，如果非要这么说的话。对。

00:29:51.170 --> 00:29:58.550
<v Diane Wu>The Luke Commission is named after Luke, from the Bible. Echo and Harry also really like the acronym, TLC.
<v Diane Wu>卢克委员会是以圣经中的卢克命名的。埃科和哈里也非常喜欢这个缩写，TLC。

00:29:58.550 --> 00:30:08.600
<v Chico Harlan>Faith-based hospitals like this one are all over sub-Saharan Africa. And just to state the obvious, Echo and Harry are the white people in a hospital that is almost entirely Black.
<v Chico Harlan>像这样的宗教医院在撒哈拉以南非洲随处可见。显而易见的是，埃科和哈里是一家几乎全部是黑人的医院里的白人。

00:30:08.600 --> 00:30:35.900
<v Diane Wu>When Harry and Echo first arrived here from Ohio in 2006, Eswatini was in crisis. Something like 30% of the population had HIV, and there was no treatment. Antiretroviral drugs had started trickling into Eswatini, but hardly anyone could get them. So many people died that one in 10 households was run by a child. Harry and Echo remember how in those early years, they saw one of their main goals as simply delaying orphanhood.
<v Diane Wu>2006年，当哈里和埃科从俄亥俄州初次来到这里时，斯威士兰正处于危机之中。大约30%的人口感染了艾滋病毒，而且没有治疗方法。抗逆转录病毒药物开始缓慢流入斯威士兰，但几乎没有人能得到它们。太多人死去，以至于十分之一的家庭由儿童支撑。哈里和埃科还记得，在那些早期，他们将主要目标之一视为单纯地推迟孤儿的诞生。

00:30:35.900 --> 00:30:58.220
<v Chico Harlan>They drove around the country with interpreters and other local staff, setting up mobile medical clinics and schools, helping people get whatever medical care they could, all for free. They paid for all this from their savings from flipping houses back in the US before they came, plus donations from family and friends. And they caravanned this way on the cheap for a couple of years till the US stepped in, in a big way.
<v Chico Harlan>他们开着车带着翻译和其他当地员工走遍全国，建立流动医疗诊所和学校，帮助人们获得他们能得到的任何医疗护理，而且全部免费。他们用离开美国之前通过炒房攒下的积蓄，加上家人和朋友的捐款来支付这一切。他们就这样以低成本结伴前行了几年，直到美国以巨大力度介入。

00:30:58.220 --> 00:31:05.180
<v George Bush>Today on the continent of Africa, nearly 30 million people have the AIDS virus.
<v George Bush>今天在非洲大陆，将近3000万感染了艾滋病毒。

00:31:05.180 --> 00:31:12.180
<v Diane Wu>This is President George W. Bush delivering the State of the Union. This is what a Republican president used to sound like.
<v Diane Wu>这是乔治·W·布什总统发表国情咨文。这就是共和党总统过去的声音。

00:31:12.180 --> 00:31:40.920
<v George Bush>Ladies and gentlemen, seldom has history offered a greater opportunity to do so much for so many. We must also remember our calling as a blessed country is to make the world better. And to meet a severe and urgent crisis abroad, tonight I propose the Emergency Plan for AIDS Relief, a work of mercy beyond all current international efforts to help the people of Africa.
<v George Bush>女士们先生们，历史极少提供如此巨大的机会，为如此多的人做这么多的事。我们还必须记住，我们作为一个受祝福国家的使命是让世界变得更美好。为了应对海外严重而紧迫的危机，今晚我提议实施“总统抗艾滋病紧急救援计划”，这是一项超越当前所有旨在帮助非洲人民的国际努力的仁慈行动。

00:31:40.920 --> 00:32:03.750
<v Diane Wu>This plan, the President's Emergency Plan for AIDS Relief, or PEPFAR, becomes one of the biggest humanitarian foreign aid projects America has ever undertaken, totaling more than $120 billion, spent all over the world over 20 years. For comparison, the Marshall Plan to rebuild Europe? Adjusted for inflation, around $180 billion.
<v Diane Wu>这项计划，即“总统抗艾滋病紧急救援计划”或称PEPFAR，成为美国有史以来承办的最大的国际人道主义援助项目之一，在20年里在全球各地总共花费了超过1200亿美元。相比之下，用于重建欧洲的马歇尔计划呢？根据通货膨胀调整后，大约是1800亿美元。

00:32:03.750 --> 00:32:22.190
<v Diane Wu>In Eswatini, PEPFAR money started arriving in 2007, some going directly to the government to support their national AIDS response, some to outside organizations. In 2010, Harry and Echo get their first contract under PEPFAR, a modest grant, doing voluntary male circumcision.
<v Diane Wu>在斯威士兰，PEPFAR资金于2007年开始到达，一部分直接流向政府以支持其国家艾滋病应对工作，一部分流向外部组织。2010年，哈里和埃科获得了他们在PEPFAR下的第一份合同，这是一笔适度的资助，用于开展自愿男性割礼。

00:32:22.190 --> 00:32:45.610
<v Echo VanderWal>They asked us to participate in that, which we were very motivated to do because it decreases the transmission and contraction of HIV by 60%. So we started, and within one month, we were circumcising more men and boys in one community than they were in the rest of the country combined. I mean, one day we did 87 circumcisions in one day. We were done by 9 o'clock at night. We are a machine.
<v Echo VanderWal>他们邀请我们参与其中，我们非常有动力去做，因为它将艾滋病毒的传播和感染率降低了60%。于是我们开始了，在一个月内，我们在一个社区为男性和男孩实施的割礼数量超过了该国其他地区总和。我是说，有一天我们在一天内完成了87台割礼。我们在晚上9点前做完。我们就是一台机器。

00:32:45.610 --> 00:32:58.030
<v Diane Wu>PEPFAR money completely changed the course of the AIDS epidemic in Eswatini. The number of people dying from AIDS went down by half. The average lifespan, which had dropped down to 41 years, fully rebounded.
<v Diane Wu>PEPFAR资金彻底改变了斯威士兰艾滋病流行的进程。死于艾滋病的人数减少了一半。曾跌至41岁的平均寿命全面反弹。

00:32:58.030 --> 00:33:27.230
<v Chico Harlan>Over time, the Luke Commission became one of the biggest recipients of American aid in the country. It averaged over $6 million a year for the last five years. And with all that money, their operations changed a lot. Echo and Harry bought this land and turned a storage shed into a ward for tuberculosis patients. And then they kept going. They got physically bigger, adding an emergency room, an ICU, a maternity ward, growing to a true campus of 25 buildings.
<v Chico Harlan>随着时间的推移，卢克委员会成为该国最大的美国援助接受者之一。在过去五年里，它平均每年获得超过600万美元。随着所有这些资金的注入，他们的运营发生了很大变化。埃科和哈里买下了这片土地，把一个储藏棚改造成了肺结核病房。然后他们继续前行。他们的物理规模不断扩大，增设了急诊室、重症监护室、产房，发展成为一个拥有25栋建筑的真正园区。

00:33:27.230 --> 00:33:49.910
<v Chico Harlan>And it wasn't just HIV care. They were jumping in and providing all kinds of treatment that the government wasn't able to. Eventually, they were providing free medical treatment for a huge swath of the country. At their peak, according to their numbers, it was one fourth of all the hospital visits in the country-- a fourth. And this giant expansion could not have happened without USAID.
<v Chico Harlan>而且不仅仅是艾滋病护理。他们挺身而出，提供政府无力提供的各种治疗。最终，他们为该国绝大部分地区提供了免费医疗。根据他们的数字，在顶峰时期，这占了该国所有医院就诊人数的四分之一——四分之一。如果没有美国国际开发署（USAID），这种巨大的扩张是不可能发生的。

00:33:49.910 --> 00:33:57.290
<v Diane Wu>And when Harry and Echo walked us around the campus, you could really see the money everywhere. It was honestly kind of posh.
<v Diane Wu>当哈里和埃科带我们参观园区时，你真的能到处看到资金的痕迹。说实话，这里甚至有点奢华。

00:33:57.290 --> 00:33:59.610
<v Echo VanderWal>This is our outpatient waiting area.
<v Echo VanderWal>这是我们的门诊候诊区。

00:33:59.610 --> 00:34:18.570
<v Diane Wu>First, all the medical stuff-- the four operating theaters, the airy ICU, a dental suite, all in beautiful condition, all almost totally empty since USAID froze their funding. Then, the bells and whistles we had not expected when visiting an aid-funded hospital in Eswatini, like--
<v Diane Wu>首先，所有的医疗设备——四个手术室、通风良好的重症监护室、牙科套房，全都状况极佳，自美国国际开发署冻结资助以来几乎全部闲置。然后，是我们访问斯威士兰受援助医院时意料之外的各种高科技设施，比如——

00:34:18.570 --> 00:34:23.980
<v Man>Welcome to our drone hangar. So this is Eswatini's very first medical drone network.
<v Man>欢迎来到我们的无人机机库。这就是斯威士兰的第一个医疗无人机网络。

00:34:23.980 --> 00:34:42.150
<v Chico Harlan>The Luke Commission has four huge 8-foot white drones that they use to quickly deliver snakebite antivenom and other medicine to rural parts of the country that are hard to drive to. They also have a room full of iPads, 600 of them, all provided by PEPFAR, plus a giant screen that tracks the real-time location of each of the devices.
<v Chico Harlan>卢克委员会拥有四架巨大的8英尺白色的无人机，他们用它们快速向车行困难的乡村地区运送蛇毒血清和其他药物。他们还有一个装满iPad的房间，足足有600台，全部由PEPFAR提供，此外还有一个大屏幕，实时追踪每台设备的定位。

00:34:42.150 --> 00:34:56.700
<v Chico Harlan>In the same room, they have a set of shiny 3D printers for making medical components. A few buildings over, in a sort of secret room they called "the vault," there were these giant futuristic vending machines that people could use to pick up their prescriptions, like an automated pharmacy.
<v Chico Harlan>在同一个房间里，他们有一套用于制造医疗部件的光鲜3D打印机。在几栋楼之外，在一个他们称之为“金库”的秘密房间里，有这些巨大的未来感自动售货机，人们可以用它们来取处方药，就像一个自动化药房。

00:34:56.700 --> 00:34:58.300
<v Diane Wu>And you guys have two?
<v Diane Wu>你们有两台？

00:34:58.300 --> 00:34:59.120
<v Echo VanderWal>No, we have three--
<v Echo VanderWal>不，我们有三台——

00:34:59.120 --> 00:34:59.620
<v Diane Wu>Three.
<v Diane Wu>三台。

00:34:59.470 --> 00:35:01.210
<v Echo VanderWal>--four, maybe five.
<v Echo VanderWal>——四台，也许五台。

00:35:01.210 --> 00:35:02.800
<v Echo VanderWal>[CHATTER]
<v Echo VanderWal>[嘈杂声]

00:35:02.800 --> 00:35:22.040
<v Diane Wu>It was all way more advanced than anything I'd ever encountered with my own health care in the US. As we walked around, I started to wonder, is this too nice? Should a foreign aid-funded hospital be so opulent? We went and asked Echo about all this, and she was actually surprised we were bringing it up at all.
<v Diane Wu>这一切比我在美国医疗保健中遇到的任何东西都要先进得多。当我们四处走动时，我开始琢磨，这会不会太好了？一家由外援资助的医院应该如此奢华吗？我们去问埃科关于这一切的看法，而她其实很惊讶我们会提起这个。

00:35:22.040 --> 00:35:35.170
<v Echo VanderWal>We do have Americans visit here all the time, and I'll be frank with you, you're the first one that's raised it. Yeah, this isn't opulent. I think it's just excellent.
<v Echo VanderWal>确实一直有美国人来这里参观，说实话，你是第一个提出这个问题的。对，这里确实算不上富丽堂皇。我觉得这里真是太棒了。

00:35:35.170 --> 00:35:51.550
<v Chico Harlan>The drones and the automated pharmacy, she explained that they'd gotten private money for those, not USAID money. And anyway, that's just a tiny part of their spending, just a couple percent. The rest went to basics like medical supplies, paying staff. As for the 3D printers--
<v Chico Harlan>无人机和自动化药房，她解释说他们为这些筹集了私人资金，而不是美国国际开发署的资金。不管怎样，那只是他们开支中极小的一部分，仅仅百分之几。其余的都用在了诸如医疗物资、支付员工工资等基本开支上。至于3D打印机——

00:35:51.550 --> 00:35:56.140
<v Echo VanderWal>Yeah, I think you'd be surprised how cheap they are. They might be $600. I don't know.
<v Echo VanderWal>对，我想你会对它们有多便宜感到惊讶。它们可能只要600美元。我不知道。

00:35:56.140 --> 00:36:27.460
<v Diane Wu>We checked. One was $600. The other, $1,400. This was USAID money. Echo estimated that these printers had so far saved them $25,000 in equipment costs. Her explanations were pretty convincing. They won us over. And she kind of got into it, looking around the room we were in, pointing out all the scrappy ways they've kept their spending down. The deck chairs on the balcony over there? Made from pallets at the oxygen plant equipment came in. The set of plush recliners at the end of the room?
<v Diane Wu>我们查过了。一台600美元。另一台1400美元。这是美国国际开发署的资金。埃科估计，这些打印机到目前为止已经为他们节省了25,000美元的设备成本。她的解释相当有说服力。我们被说服了。她也来了兴致，环顾我们所在的房间，指出他们保持低开支的各种精打细算的办法。那边阳台上的躺椅？是用运送氧气厂设备的托盘做的。房间尽头的一套毛绒躺椅？

00:36:27.460 --> 00:36:31.260
<v Echo VanderWal>Those were from one of the first circumcision projects.
<v Echo VanderWal>那些来自最早的割礼项目之一。

00:36:31.260 --> 00:36:33.100
<v Diane Wu>You sat in it to get a circumcision?
<v Diane Wu>你坐在那上面做割礼吗？

00:36:33.100 --> 00:36:35.580
<v Echo VanderWal>After you got circumcised, you relaxed in those chairs.
<v Echo VanderWal>做完割礼后，你就在那些椅子上放松。

00:36:35.580 --> 00:36:37.390
<v Harry VanderWal>Relax and spread your legs out a little bit.
<v Harry VanderWal>放松并稍微把腿张开。

00:36:37.390 --> 00:36:38.430
<v Echo VanderWal>Oh, Harry!
<v Echo VanderWal>哦，哈里！

00:36:38.430 --> 00:36:39.780
<v Harry VanderWal>[LAUGHS]
<v Harry VanderWal>[笑声]

00:36:39.780 --> 00:36:43.270
<v Diane Wu>I explained to Echo, as I followed her around campus--
<v Diane Wu>我向埃科解释，当我跟着她在园区四处走动时——

00:36:43.270 --> 00:37:00.810
<v Diane Wu>I was picturing, like, what if Marco Rubio were on this tour? Like, what would he see? What would his reaction be? And my thought was like, American taxpayer dollars are going to hundreds of iPads in Eswatini for AIDS?
<v Diane Wu>我在想象，比如，如果马可·鲁比奥参加这次参观会怎么样？比如，他会看到什么？他的反应会是什么？我的想法是，美国纳税人的钱竟然流向了斯威士兰的数百台iPad用来治艾滋病？

00:37:00.810 --> 00:37:31.360
<v Echo VanderWal>I feel if Marco Rubio came here, and he knew that they funded 600 iPads, and he found all the iPads here, and it allows you to see twice as many people with half as many staff, and he found that there's a system that when you leave the gate, if you're working out with an iPad that's supposed to be used for patient care, that there's a way-- there's a control that makes sure that that asset is doing what it's supposed to do, I just-- I think those are all things that would be cham--
<v Echo VanderWal>我觉得如果马可·鲁比奥来到这里，他知道他们资助了600台iPad，并且他在这里找到了所有的iPad，并且这能让你用一半的员工看到两倍多的人，而且他发现有这样一个系统：当你离开大门时，如果你带着本应用於患者护理的iPad外出，有一种方法——有一种控制方法可以确保该资产正在做它应该做的事情，我只是——我认为这些都是将要改变的……

00:37:31.360 --> 00:37:33.150
<v Diane Wu>That Marco Rubio would be into?
<v Diane Wu>马可·鲁比奥会感兴趣的？

00:37:33.150 --> 00:37:41.950
<v Echo VanderWal>I think he would be.
<v Echo VanderWal>我想他会的。

00:37:41.950 --> 00:38:03.340
<v Chico Harlan>After USAID abruptly stopped its programs in January, the Luke Commission shut down everything to the bare essentials. Harry and Echo felt a moral obligation to keep giving out antiretroviral and tuberculosis drugs to HIV patients. So they continued that work. They still did some snake bites-- they're the main hospital in the country that treats them-- and an occasional surgery.
<v Chico Harlan>在1月份美国国际开发署突然叫停其项目后，卢克委员会将一切精简到了维持基本需求的程度。哈里和埃科感到有一种道德义务，要继续向艾滋病毒患者发放抗逆转录病毒药物和结核病药物。所以他们继续了这项工作。他们仍然处理一些蛇咬伤——他们是该国治疗蛇咬伤的主力医院——以及偶尔的手术。

00:38:03.340 --> 00:38:26.670
<v Chico Harlan>But they also laid off half their staff and told everyone else who kept working that they could no longer afford to pay them. USAID covered half of their cash flow, and now it was gone. They couldn't even pay their utility bills. And, for the first time, they started turning people away. Workers put up the gate we saw. Confused patients called the Luke Commission to ask what's happening.
<v Chico Harlan>但他们也解雇了一半的员工，并告诉所有继续工作的其他人，他们再也付不起工资了。美国国际开发署承担了他们一半的现金流，现在这笔钱没了。他们甚至连水电费都交不起了。而且，他们破天荒地开始将人拒之门外。工人们竖起了我们看到的那个大门。困惑的患者打电话给卢克委员会询问发生了什么。

00:38:26.670 --> 00:38:43.770
<v Diane Wu>We talked to two people who answered those calls, Nompumelelo and her co-worker, Tanele. They were at work, sitting at a big gray folding table, four phones between the two of them. Nompumelelo said they'd tell people on the phone that they weren't open anymore, and they'd show up anyway.
<v Diane Wu>我们与两名接听这些电话的人进行了交谈，诺姆普梅莱洛和她的同事塔内莱。她们正在上班，坐在一张灰色的大折叠桌旁，两人共用四部电话。诺姆普梅莱洛说，她们会在电话里告诉人们他们不再营业了，但人们还是会找上门来。

00:38:43.770 --> 00:38:52.960
<v Nompumelelo>Some still don't believe that we are really closed, so they want to come and see themselves.
<v Nompumelelo>有些人仍然不相信我们真的关门了，所以他们想亲自来看看。

00:38:52.960 --> 00:39:07.680
<v Tanele>They are like Thomas, the Thomas in the Bible. Do you not believe when they say Jesus has risen? I want to see myself. [LAUGHS] So even some-- even the ones that are calling, most of them-- is it true that TLC is closed? How can that be?
<v Tanele>他们就像多马，圣经里的那个多马。当别人说耶稣复活了，你难道不相信吗？我要亲眼看看。[笑声]所以甚至有些人——连那些打电话的人，大多数都在问——TLC真的关门了吗？怎么会这样？

00:39:07.680 --> 00:39:35.770
<v Diane Wu>Tanele is in her 20s, in a red Luke Commission T-shirt and pencil skirt, and just sort of beams earnestness at you. On your 21st birthday in Eswatini, there's this thing where parents give their kids a key, symbolizing you're an adult. Be free. Do what you want. Her parents gave her a Bible, which she laughed about. Her co-worker, Nompumelelo, is older, and extremely soft-spoken, which actually got her the job answering phones.
<v Diane Wu>塔内莱二十多岁，穿着红色的卢克委员会T恤和铅笔裙，向你散发着一种真诚。在斯威士兰，当你满21岁时，父母会给孩子们一把钥匙，象征着你已经是成年人了。去自由吧。去做你想做的事。她的父母送了她一本圣经，她对此笑了笑。她的同事诺姆普梅莱洛年龄稍长，说话极其轻声细语，这其实正是她被录用接听电话的原因。

00:39:35.770 --> 00:39:42.360
<v Nompumelelo>They say my voice is soft. [LAUGHS] So they said I should assist her.
<v Nompumelelo>他们说我的声音很温柔。[笑声]所以他们说我应该去协助她。

00:39:42.360 --> 00:39:44.140
<v Diane Wu>Because you have a nice voice?
<v Diane Wu>因为你声音好听？

00:39:44.140 --> 00:39:50.850
<v Nompumelelo>[CHUCKLES] No, because of my personality and also the voice. So yes.
<v Nompumelelo>[轻笑]不，是因为我的性格还有声音。所以是的。

00:39:50.850 --> 00:40:18.620
<v Diane Wu>Now that the Luke Commission is closed, the tens of thousands of patients they served have to find other health care. We heard some pretty grim stories about the treatment people received in the national health care system-- a misdiagnosis, a botched surgery. But the biggest problem people told us about was the lack of medicine and supplies. So when Nompumelelo and Tanele turn people away, the patients cry on the phone and plead for them to go and talk to the doctors, try to get them in.
<v Diane Wu>现在卢克委员会关门了，他们服务过的数万名患者不得不寻找其他医疗途径。我们听到了一些关于人们在国家医疗系统中接受治疗的严峻故事——误诊、失败的手术。但人们告诉我们的最大问题是缺乏药物和物资。因此，当诺姆普梅莱洛和塔内莱将患者拒之门外时，患者们在电话里哭泣，恳求她们去和医生谈谈，尽量把他们收治进去。

00:40:18.620 --> 00:40:35.560
<v Nompumelelo>And then when the doctor still says no, it's hard to go back to the patient and tell them, ish, because you know-- you feel what they are feeling. Due to the current situation, we can't help them. Ish. [SIGHS]
<v Nompumelelo>然后当医生仍然说不时，很难再回到患者面前对他们说，唉，因为你知道——你感同身受着他们的感受。由于目前的情况，我们无能为力。唉。[叹气]

00:40:35.560 --> 00:40:40.630
<v Diane Wu>Has anybody expressed anger at the American government for taking the money away?
<v Diane Wu>有没有人对美国政府撤回资金表达愤怒？

00:40:40.630 --> 00:40:49.900
<v Nompumelelo>The anger is not-- it's not towards the US government. But they are angry at our government--
<v Nompumelelo>愤怒不是——不是针对美国政府。但他们对我们的政府感到愤怒——

00:40:49.900 --> 00:40:51.340
<v Tanele>True.
<v Tanele>确实。

00:40:51.340 --> 00:40:52.730
<v Nompumelelo>--for not helping.
<v Nompumelelo>——因为没有提供帮助。

00:40:52.730 --> 00:41:00.700
<v Tanele>True, and that also that our government-- they know that our government is dragging feet to assist financially.
<v Tanele>确实，而且还有我们的政府——他们知道我们的政府在财政援助方面拖后腿。

00:41:00.700 --> 00:41:26.120
<v Chico Harlan>Almost everyone we asked in Eswatini, people with and without ties to The Luke Commission said the same thing. They weren't mad at the US for cutting off aid. Here's Colani. He's a security guard who stands at the new gate they built. He had to turn people away, which he hated doing. He lost both his parents early in the AIDS epidemic. He told us he wishes a government official could stand at the gate with him and see all the people who aren't getting care.
<v Chico Harlan>我们在斯威士兰询问的几乎所有人，无论与卢克委员会有没有联系的人，都说了同样的话。他们并没有对美国切断援助感到愤怒。这是科拉尼。他是站在他们新建的大门旁的保安。他不得不把人拒之门外，而他最讨厌这么做。他在艾滋病流行的早期失去了双亲。他告诉我们，他希望政府官员能和他一起站在大门旁，看看所有得不到治疗的人。

00:41:26.120 --> 00:42:00.440
<v Colani>The anger from my-- personally, it's actually at our own government. Why isn't our government intervening into this situation? That is where the frustration comes from, not the USAID. Of course, I mean, when you've always known that this person has always supported me, you'll get disappointed that, oh, why are they now leaving me? But we have our own government within our country that should actually be looking after us, you know?
<v Colani>我的愤怒——就我个人而言，其实是针对我们自己的政府。为什么我们的政府不干涉这种情况？挫败感由此产生，而不是对美国国际开发署。当然，我的意思是，当你一直都知道这个人一直在支持你时，你会感到失望，哦，为什么他们现在要抛弃我？但我们自己国家内有政府，本该真正照顾我们才对，你知道吗？

00:42:00.440 --> 00:42:19.150
<v Diane Wu>The government of Eswatini, by the way, is an absolute monarchy. King Mswati III has a lavish lifestyle-- famously gave each of his many wives a Rolls-Royce. No one inside the country who we interviewed put the blame on him or said anything bad about him. It's not prudent to criticize the king if you live there.
<v Diane Wu>顺便说一句，斯威士兰政府是一个绝对君主制国家。姆斯瓦蒂三世国王生活奢靡——最为人熟知的是他给他的多位妻子每人送了一辆劳斯莱斯。我们在国内采访的任何人都没有将责任归咎于他，也没有说他半句坏话。如果你住在那里，批评国王是不明智的。

00:42:19.150 --> 00:42:39.310
<v Diane Wu>Spending time in Eswatini made me realize a monarchy is basically a dictatorship with better branding. But dissidents living abroad say the king's patronage system fosters a culture of corruption throughout the government, including in the Ministry of Health. And when people talked to us, those were the officials they focused on.
<v Diane Wu>在斯威士兰呆上一段时间让我意识到，君主制基本上就是贴着更好包装的独裁统治。但流亡海外的异见人士表示，国王的庇护体制在整个政府（包括卫生部）中助长了一种腐败文化。当人们与我们交谈时，那些官员正是他们关注的焦点。

00:42:39.310 --> 00:42:59.650
<v Colani>I'll be honest. There's quite a lot of corruption, I'll be honest, in our health sector. There's a certain group of people that are trying, by all means, to ensure that the government facilities are not operating well so that we go and buy medication from their pharmacies.
<v Colani>说实话。我们的卫生部门存在相当多的腐败，说实话。有一群人正千方百计地确保政府医疗机构无法正常运转，这样我们就会去他们的药房购买药物。

00:42:59.650 --> 00:43:29.000
<v Chico Harlan>Colani is talking about something that is well-documented, that a group of government officials have allegedly been colluding with drug suppliers and causing shortages in the country. The Eswatini government launched a big investigation a couple of years ago and found that officials were systematically buying drugs that were about to expire and getting kickbacks from the suppliers. Then patients, they had to scramble to find drugs at pharmacies, paying for drugs that they should have gotten for free. The Ministry of Health declined to comment about all this.
<v Chico Harlan>科拉尼谈论的是一个有据可查的事实，据称有一群政府官员一直在与药物供应商勾结，导致该国药品短缺。斯威士兰政府在几年前展开了一项大规模调查，发现官员们系统性地购买即将过期的药物并从供应商那里拿回扣。接着，患者们不得不四处奔走在药房找药，为本应免费获得的药物自掏腰包。卫生部拒绝就这一切发表评论。

00:43:29.000 --> 00:43:52.470
<v Diane Wu>And the reason we're going into all this detail is that it shows you one of the big ways US taxpayer dollars may have made things worse in Eswatini. By building an alternative health system that worked really well, it made it easier for corruption to grow in the government system. Harry and Echo have been thinking about that a lot in the last few months since the Luke Commission closed its gates. Maybe they were part of the problem.
<v Diane Wu>我们之所以要讲这么多细节，是因为它展示了美国纳税人的资金可能在斯威士兰使情况变得更糟的一个主要方式。通过建立一个运行得非常良好的替代卫生系统，它使得政府体制内腐败更容易滋生。在卢克诊所关闭大门的过去几个月里，哈里和埃cho一直在深入思考这个问题。也许他们也是问题的一部分。

00:43:52.470 --> 00:44:06.910
<v Harry VanderWal>And maybe all the help that we've been providing over these past years during COVID was actually doing more damage than good, allowing this monster to get fatter and fatter, because as we did more and more and more, the demand on the national health care system that ought to have been crumbling was becoming less and less.
<v Harry VanderWal>也许我们在过去这些年新冠疫情期间提供的所有帮助，实际上造成的破坏大于益处，让这个怪兽变得越来越胖，因为随着我们做得越来越多，本该崩溃的国家医疗保健系统的需求却变得越来越少。

00:44:06.910 --> 00:44:24.930
<v Harry VanderWal>And so the cries of people was not as much as it should have been to make the changes that should have happened long ago, because we were busy covering up, in some ways. By doing-- working so hard and doing good, we were covering up the evil that was actually happening under the national health care system.
<v Harry VanderWal>因此，人们的呼声没有达到应有的程度，未能推动本该在很久以前就发生的变革，因为我们在某种程度上忙于粉饰。通过努力工作和行善，我们掩盖了国家医疗保健系统下实际发生的邪恶。

00:44:24.930 --> 00:44:46.570
<v Chico Harlan>Harry and Echo say a turning point came during COVID, when the Luke Commission handled so many of the critical care cases for the country. In the aftermath, with demand still rising, Harry and Echo took out a loan against their house and emptied their retirement savings to pay staff. They asked the Eswatini government to help cover the cost of treating so many patients and assumed that the money would come through at some point.
<v Chico Harlan>哈里和埃cho说，一个转折点出现在新冠疫情期间，当时卢克诊所处理了该国大量的重症监护病例。事后，随着需求持续上升，哈里和埃cho用他们的房子抵押贷款，并掏空了退休储蓄来支付员工工资。他们请求斯威士兰政府协助承担治疗如此多患者的费用，并认为这笔资金最终会拨付到位。

00:44:46.570 --> 00:44:59.640
<v Harry VanderWal>I just wish we would have maybe been not as naive, I guess, or not as-- maybe made these changes a couple of years ahead or made some lines in the sand earlier, maybe.
<v Harry VanderWal>我只是希望我们当初也许没那么天真，我想，或者没有——也许提前几年做出这些改变，或者早点划清底线，也许吧。

00:44:59.640 --> 00:45:19.960
<v Chico Harlan>They never got the money. The health minister at the time says the government offered the Luke Commission annual guaranteed funding, but only if it had some oversight over how the money was spent. Any government would probably ask for the same thing. In this case, she told me that Eswatini wanted to name the chairman of the Luke Commission's board as a way to oversee their operations.
<v Chico Harlan>他们从未拿到这笔钱。当时的卫生部长表示，政府曾向卢克诊所提供年度资金担保，但前提是政府必须对资金的使用方式拥有一定的监督权。任何政府大概都会提出同样的要求。她告诉我，在这种情况下，斯威士兰希望由卢克诊所董事会主席来兼任，以此作为监督其运营的一种方式。

00:45:19.960 --> 00:45:38.830
<v Chico Harlan>Harry and Echo say this was never actually proposed to them, but if it had been, they would have said no. From their point of view, that would be like a hostile takeover. Before long, they'd get booted out, the government would assume control, and the Luke Commission would become just another unreliable hospital in a system that can't stock its own drugs.
<v Chico Harlan>哈里和埃cho表示，对方从未向他们真正提出过这个要求，但如果提了，他们也会拒绝。在他们看来，这就好要像是一次恶意收购。不久之后，他们就会被踢出局，政府将接管控制权，而卢克诊所将沦为这个无法自行储备药品的系统中又一个不可靠的医院。

00:45:38.830 --> 00:46:08.390
<v Chico Harlan>The standoff between the two sides got so nasty that it became the basis of a 2024 parliamentary report to investigate the, quote, "bottlenecks and disharmony." One of the findings said senior health officials viewed Echo as, quote, "a difficult person to work with." Another finding? Quote, "There are very senior individuals in government who are hell bent on blocking any assistance to the Luke Commission for their own selfish reasons that are contrary to the wellness of the sick, the weak, and the elderly in our country."
<v Chico Harlan>双方的僵局变得如此恶劣，以至于成为20th2024年议会调查报告的基础，该报告旨在调查所谓的“瓶颈与不和谐”。其中一项调查结果显示，高级卫生官员认为埃cho是“一个很难合作的人”。另一个调查结果是？引文：“政府中有极高级别的个人，出于他们自己的自私原因，死板地阻挠对卢克诊所的任何援助，这违背了我国病弱和老人的健康。”

00:46:08.390 --> 00:46:29.270
<v Chico Harlan>Suffice to say, it's messy and unresolved. These are the strange and unpredictable consequences of sending hundreds of millions of American dollars to Eswatini. These Christian do-gooders are now in a bitter fight against officials in the government of the people they came to help.
<v Chico Harlan>可以说，情况既混乱又未解决。这些是将数亿美元美国资金送往斯威士兰所带来的奇怪且不可预测的后果。这些基督教慈善人士现在正与他们前来帮助的人民的政府官员展开苦战。

00:46:29.270 --> 00:46:45.240
<v Diane Wu>Meanwhile, HIV, the disease they came here to treat 20 years ago, it's much more under control now. And even Harry and Echo wonder why the United States is still in the HIV business here. Why are US taxpayers still paying such a big part of the bill for that?
<v Diane Wu>与此同时，他们20年前来到这里治疗的疾病——艾滋病，现在得到了更好的控制。甚至连哈里和埃cho也在纳闷，为什么美国现在还要在这里从事艾滋病业务？为什么美国纳税人仍然在为这笔账支付如此大的一部分？

00:46:45.240 --> 00:47:01.960
<v Echo VanderWal>We haven't transitioned-- just look at HIV. We have not transitioned like we should. This is an old disease. By now, we should have transitioned it to being locally supported so that global aid can prepare itself or ready itself for the next global pandemic or problem, whatever that is. We don't know.
<v Echo VanderWal>我们没有转型——看看艾滋病就知道了。我们没有像应有的那样进行转型。这是一种古老的疾病。到现在为止，我们本应将它过渡到由地方支持，以便全球援助能够为下一次全球大流行或问题做好准备，无论那是什么。我们不知道。

00:47:01.960 --> 00:47:06.900
<v Diane Wu>And for HIV, if that had happened, when would the transition have happened here?
<v Diane Wu>对于艾滋病来说，如果发生了那种情况，这里的转型会是什么时候？

00:47:06.900 --> 00:47:17.250
<v Echo VanderWal>It probably should have happened five years ago. I think it should have happened before now. And it could have. It could have happened here, and it probably could have happened in other countries.
<v Echo VanderWal>它可能应该在五年前发生。我认为它应该在现在之前发生。而且本可以发生。它本可以在这里发生，而且可能在其他国家也发生过。

00:47:17.250 --> 00:47:29.520
<v Harry VanderWal>For me, I think the challenge is, as Americans, too, we want to fix things. We want to run in and fix it. Sometimes we have to be careful on what's our exit strategy. How do we-- once we jump in, how do we get out?
<v Harry VanderWal>对我们来说，我认为挑战在于，作为美国人，我们也想解决问题。我们想冲进去解决问题。有时我们必须谨慎对待我们的退出策略是什么。一旦我们跳进去，我们该如何脱身？

00:47:29.520 --> 00:47:52.960
<v Diane Wu>Someone in Eswatini who's been thinking about how to transition away from foreign aid for over 20 years now is Dr. Velephi Okello, the director of Health Services in the Ministry of Health. She oversaw the long process of getting the government of Eswatini to pay for all of its adult antiretroviral drugs, something they've been doing for 10 years now. She was part of the team figuring out how things would work after PEPFAR.
<v Diane Wu>斯威士兰有一位已经思考如何摆脱外国援助20多年的人，她就是卫生部卫生服务总监维莱菲·奥凯洛博士。她监督了让斯威士兰政府为所有成年人抗逆转录病毒药物买单的漫长过程，这件事他们已经做了10年。她是弄清楚PEPFAR（美国总统艾滋病紧急救援计划）之后事情将如何运作的团队成员之一。

00:47:52.960 --> 00:47:56.770
<v Velephi Okello>We thought that the PEPFAR funds would take us to 2030.
<v Velephi Okello>我们以为PEPFAR的资金会支持我们到2030年。

00:47:56.770 --> 00:47:57.760
<v Diane Wu>2030.
<v Diane Wu>2030.

00:47:57.760 --> 00:48:04.570
<v Velephi Okello>And then we're able to slowly and gently offload some of the issues back to government.
<v Velephi Okello>然后我们能够缓慢而温和地将一些问题交还给政府。

00:48:04.570 --> 00:48:10.010
<v Diane Wu>Then, in January, she heard that President Trump had suddenly halted the USAID money.
<v Diane Wu>接着，在1月份，她听说特朗普总统突然叫停了美国国际开发署（USAID）的资金。

00:48:10.010 --> 00:48:39.250
<v Velephi Okello>So when I saw it, I thought, ah, Trump is just doing his usual-- what do you call-- being a [LAUGHS] spontaneous kind of thinking, if I may put it like that. So I thought, what does this mean? And we consulted our colleagues from PEPFAR. Then they told us, ah, no, this is real, and there's more coming. So we said, OK, fasten your seat belts. This is happening. What other rough ride are we going to have?
<v Velephi Okello>所以当我看到它时，我想，啊，特朗普只是在做他惯常的——你叫它什么——那种即兴的想法，如果我可以这样表达的话。所以我想，这代表什么？我们咨询了我们在PEPFAR的同事。然后他们告诉我们，啊，不，这是真的，还有更多的大动作要来。所以我们说，好吧，系好安全带。事情正在发生。我们还要经历什么别的艰难险阻？

00:48:39.250 --> 00:49:02.130
<v Diane Wu>She sent a team of people out to assess the damage. Where exactly was all this PEPFAR money being used, and how much? Her initial assessment is that the situation is not so bad. Eswatini's been buying all its own antiretrovirals for adults for years, and they have enough pediatric antiretroviral drugs in the pipeline. They know which lab reagents need funding quickly to keep testing people.
<v Diane Wu>她派出一个团队去评估损失。所有这些PEPFAR资金到底用在了哪里，用了多少？她初步评估认为情况并没有那么糟糕。斯威士兰多年来一直在购买自己的全部成人抗逆转录病毒药物，并且他们的储备中有足够的儿童抗逆转录病毒药物。他们知道哪些实验室试剂需要快速获得资金以继续对人们进行检测。

00:49:02.130 --> 00:49:22.380
<v Diane Wu>She says 20 years of American aid has made Eswatini's health system stronger in ways that will persist. It trained nurses and other medical staff, developed more effective ways to distribute antiretroviral drugs, just generally raised standards for care. She sees the health system as fairly sturdy at this point, able to care for its own people.
<v Diane Wu>她表示，20年的美国援助以将持续发挥作用的方式增强了斯威士兰的卫生系统。它培训了护士和其他医务人员，开发了更有效的抗逆转录病毒药物分发方法，总体上提高了护理标准。她认为目前的卫生系统相当稳固，能够照顾自己的人民。

00:49:22.380 --> 00:49:42.540
<v Velephi Okello>I always say to people, our health system is like a-- let me say a Toyota. [CHUCKLES] We are using a model that is basic, basic health service delivery model. It's moving and maybe not so comfortable, but it gets you there.
<v Velephi Okello>我经常对人们说，我们的卫生系统就像一个——比方说一辆丰田汽车。[轻笑] 我们使用的是一种基础的、基础的卫生服务交付模型。它在行驶，也许没那么舒适，但它能把你送到目的地。

00:49:42.540 --> 00:50:02.980
<v Velephi Okello>So when PEPFAR came, we always say that they took us from that basic Toyota into some kind of Mercedes Benz or Rolls-Royce, which now made sure that our quality of services is great. And we are moving faster in achieving the targets that we had aimed to achieve.
<v Velephi Okello>所以当PEPFAR到来时，我们总是说他们把我们从那辆基础的丰田车带到了某种奔驰或劳斯莱斯里，这现在确保了我们的服务质量是一流的。我们在实现我们旨在实现的目标方面正在更快地前进。

00:50:02.980 --> 00:50:08.050
<v Diane Wu>The Mercedes is gone now, but she thinks they'll still get to where they need to go.
<v Diane Wu>奔驰现在已经没了，但她认为他们仍然能到达他们需要去的地方。

00:50:08.050 --> 00:50:21.700
<v Chico Harlan>Dr. Okello, of course, works for the government. A UNAIDS assessment that came out recently-- not so rosy. It said there is a risk of running out of antiretroviral drugs and other supplies in three to six months in Eswatini.
<v Chico Harlan>奥凯洛博士当然是为政府工作的。最近发布的一份联合国艾滋病规划署（UNAIDS）评估报告——情况并不那么乐观。它指出，斯威士兰在三到六个月内存在抗逆转录病毒药物及其他物资耗尽的风险。

00:50:21.700 --> 00:50:45.940
<v Chico Harlan>And while Eswatini does pay for its own antiretrovirals, it takes a lot more than drugs to combat HIV. It's a chronic illness where so much of the work involves constant monitoring for new cases and maintaining a network of clinics and community health workers. So people have the drugs they need and are taking them every day. In Eswatini, lots of that work was still paid for by foreign money, mostly American.
<v Chico Harlan>虽然斯威士兰确实支付了自身的抗逆转录病毒药物费用，但抗击艾滋病需要的远不止药物。这是一种慢性病，其中很大一部分工作涉及对新病例的持续监测，以及维护诊所和社区卫生工作者的网络。这样人们就能拥有他们需要的药物并且每天都在服用。在斯威士兰，这其中的许多工作仍然是由外国资金支付的，主要是美国的资金。

00:50:45.940 --> 00:51:06.980
<v Chico Harlan>Some PEPFAR funding may survive. They're debating that right now on Capitol Hill. But even if nothing is restored, Eswatini is in a better position than most PEPFAR countries in Africa to take over that work. It has a bunch of advantages. It's small. It has a fair amount of money. And it's done a good job to mobilize and control HIV as a national priority for decades.
<v Chico Harlan>部分PEPFAR资金可能会幸存。国会山目前正在对此进行辩论。但即使没有恢复任何资金，斯威士兰在接管这项工作方面也比非洲的大多数PEPFAR国家处于更有利的地位。它具备一系列优势。它很小。它拥有相当数量的资金。几十年来，它在将艾滋病作为国家首要任务进行动员和控制方面做得很好。

00:51:06.980 --> 00:51:48.650
<v Chico Harlan>One of the people who originally created PEPFAR told us, out of the 50 PEPFAR countries worldwide, maybe a dozen are ready to move off the program's money fairly quickly. And Eswatini is one of them. He also told us, PEPFAR was never intended to last this long. The conversations about how to wind down had already started in the Bush years, but nobody carried them through. Once a big bureaucracy took shape around this money in the US and abroad, there wasn't much momentum to uproot everything. That is, of course, until now.
<v Chico Harlan>当初创建PEPFAR的人之一告诉我们，在全球的50个PEPFAR国家中，也许有十几个国家已经准备好相当迅速地退出该项目的资金支持。斯威士兰就是其中之一。他还告诉我们，PEPFAR从一开始就没打算持续这么长时间。关于如何逐步缩减规模的讨论在布什执政时期就已经开始了，但没有人坚持贯彻到底。一旦围绕这笔资金在美国国内和海外形成庞大的官僚机构，就没有多少动力去根除一切了。当然，这种情况一直持续到现在。

00:51:48.650 --> 00:52:09.720
<v Ira Glass>Chico Harlan is a reporter at The Washington Post. He also did a great print version of this story. Diane Wu is one of the producers of our show. By the way, since they visited, the Luke Commission has opened up its gates again, at a much smaller scale. To pay for that, they're charging for their services for the first time.
<v Ira Glass>奇科·哈兰是《华盛顿邮报》的一名记者。他还为这个故事做了一个出色的印刷版。黛安·吴是我们节目的制作人之一。顺便说一句，自他们访问以来，卢克诊所以较小规模重新开放了大门。为了支付费用，他们首次开始对服务收费。

00:52:09.720 --> 00:52:39.840
<v Ira Glass>So how bad is this going to be, the US pulling back from all this humanitarian aid? Well, in March, a USAID employee made a memo public on his way out the door, where the agency estimated that there would be 200,000 more children paralyzed with polio each year, 18 million extra cases of malaria each year. Secretary of State Marco Rubio has said that nobody's died as a result of the freeze on USAID. That seems not to be true. Reporters have found examples-- real people.
<v Ira Glass>那么，美国撤回所有这些人道主义援助会有多糟糕？嗯，三月份，一名美国国际开发署员工在离职时公开了一份备忘录，该机构在备忘录中估计，每年将有20万名更多的儿童因小儿麻痹症瘫痪，每年还会多出1800万例疟疾病例。国务卿马可·鲁比奥曾表示，没有人因冻结美国国际开发署资金而死亡。这似乎并非事实。记者们已经找到了实例——活生生的人。

00:52:39.840 --> 00:52:59.720
<v Ira Glass>But how many people are likely to die? There are different estimates. The most thorough one we found was done by the Center for Global Development. My coworker, David Kestenbaum, talked with Charles Kenny, who worked on some of the modeling. Kenny said that some of the first deaths likely to show up would be from the thing we've been talking about, cuts to HIV programs.
<v Ira Glass>但是究竟有多少人可能会死呢？有不同的估计。我们找到的最彻底的评估是由全球发展中心完成的。我的同事大卫·克斯滕鲍姆与参与了一些建模工作的查尔斯·肯尼进行了交谈。肯尼说，可能显现的第一批死亡病例将来自于我们一直在讨论的事情——对艾滋病项目的削减。

00:52:59.720 --> 00:53:10.580
<v Charles Kenny>We have a pretty good knowledge of how many people and how quickly die-- how quickly they die, if you take them off antiretroviral medications.
<v Charles Kenny>对于如果不让人们服用抗逆转录病毒药物，会有多少人死亡以及死亡速度有多快，我们有相当深入的了解。

00:53:10.580 --> 00:53:19.870
<v Ira Glass>So they went through the actual contracts that got cut in the government database and found that about 18% of the money for these programs had been eliminated. And they ran the numbers.
<v Ira Glass>因此，他们查阅了政府数据库中被削减的实际合同，发现这些项目大约18%的资金已被取消。他们运行了这些数据。

00:53:19.870 --> 00:53:28.780
<v Charles Kenny>The modeled estimate is that 200,000 or so people will die due to the cuts to awards this year.
<v Charles Kenny>根据模型估算，今年由于对资助的削减，将有大约20万左右的人死亡。

00:53:28.780 --> 00:53:46.690
<v Ira Glass>He says it is possible that individual countries' governments will step in, at least to some extent. But because of the abruptness of the cuts, the way the United States just froze funding when President Trump came in, without giving countries time to prepare and take over those health services, he says, there's no way that's going to happen fast enough.
<v Ira Glass>他说，个别国家的政府有可能会进行干预，至少在某种程度上。但由于削减的突然性，即特朗普总统上台后美国直接冻结资金的方式，没有给各国留出时间来准备和接管这些医疗服务，他说，这是绝对不可能以足够快的速度发生的。

00:53:46.690 --> 00:53:58.410
<v Charles Kenny>While I think it is probably fair to say that 200,000 estimate isn't going to be true in the long-term, in the short-term, I think it's probably horribly close to true.
<v Charles Kenny>虽然我认为可以说20万的估计长期来看不会成真，但在短期内，我认为它可能极其接近真实数字。

00:53:58.410 --> 00:54:09.080
<v Ira Glass>And that's just from HIV programs. There were cuts to malaria prevention, tuberculosis, childhood vaccines. Adding it all up, Kenny says it could be over a half million deaths each year.
<v Ira Glass>而这仅仅来自艾滋病项目。还有对疟疾预防、结核病、儿童疫苗的削减。把所有这些加起来，肯尼说每年可能会导致超过五十万人死亡。

00:54:09.080 --> 00:54:29.210
<v Charles Kenny>It's really depressing because you can see the trends in HIV deaths and malaria deaths, and they were going down. They were dramatically down. And that is an amazing effort. And the US is leading it. And that does make all of this more tragic.
<v Charles Kenny>这真的很让人沮丧，因为你可以看到艾滋病死亡人数和疟疾死亡人数的趋势，它们曾经在下降。它们在大幅下降。这是一项惊人的努力。而美国正引领着它。这确实让这一切变得更加悲惨。

00:54:29.210 --> 00:54:33.980
<v David Kestenbaum>How does it feel to run the numbers?
<v David Kestenbaum>运行这些数字是什么感觉？

00:54:33.980 --> 00:54:41.180
<v Charles Kenny>It's easier to think of them as just numbers, eh? [CRYING]
<v Charles Kenny>把它们仅仅当作数字来想会容易些，对吧？[哭泣]

00:54:41.180 --> 00:54:44.480
<v David Kestenbaum>Are you crying now? Sorry.
<v David Kestenbaum>你现在在哭吗？对不起。

00:54:44.480 --> 00:54:46.750
<v Charles Kenny>Yes, I am.
<v Charles Kenny>是的，我在哭。

00:54:46.750 --> 00:54:49.800
<v David Kestenbaum>[BREATHES DEEPLY] I'm sorry.
<v David Kestenbaum>[深呼吸] 对不起。

00:54:49.800 --> 00:54:58.110
<v Charles Kenny>No, no. It is a completely legitimate question.
<v Charles Kenny>不，不。这是一个完全合理的问题。

00:54:58.110 --> 00:55:16.170
<v Ira Glass>Whether or not you think the United States should stay in the business of doing things like providing HIV drugs around the world, the way it suddenly stopped in January--without warning, without giving countries time to take over those services-- has had such a profound impact that it's hard not to wonder: Did anybody consider the cost in human lives of doing it this way? If they did, why is this the path they chose? Or did they not care enough to even ask the question? We asked for an interview with anybody at the State Department or the White House who could explain why it was done this way, and they turned us down.
<v Ira Glass>无论你是否认为美国应该继续在全球范围内做提供艾滋病药物之类的事情，今年1月份它突然停止的方式——毫无预警，没有给各国留出时间来接管这些服务——产生了如此深远的影响，以至于人们很难不去想：有没有人考虑过以这种方式行事的生命代价？如果有，为什么这是他们选择的路径？还是他们根本不在乎到甚至连这个问题都不去问？我们请求采访国务院或白宫中任何能解释为什么会以这种方式行事的人，而他们拒绝了我们。

00:55:16.170 --> 00:55:39.930
<v Ira Glass>When a few producers on our staff called around, in country after country, we kept finding people who told us about major disruptions in service. One researcher, Susan Hillis, has been asking people to record voice memos about what they're experiencing. She got this one from a man named Jay, somebody who took antiretroviral drugs every day. He's a student, a tour guide. He recorded this at home one night.
<v Ira Glass>当我们节目组的几位制作人四处打电话时，在一个又一个国家，我们不断发现有人告诉我们服务中断的严重情况。一位研究人员苏珊·希利斯一直在请人们录制关于他们正在经历的情况的语音备忘录。她收到了一个名叫杰伊的男子的语音，他是一个每天服用抗逆转录病毒药物的人。他是一名学生，也是一名导游。这是他一天晚上在家里录的。

00:55:39.930 --> 00:56:18.720
<v Jay>Hi, I'm Jay. I'm turning 24 this June, and I live in Mombasa. I have been taking the medication my whole life. I felt like it was really hard for me when the funding was stopped, and I went to the hospital and I couldn't find medicine. So I would go, be told come next week, come next week, and I wouldn't find medicine.
<v Jay>嗨，我是杰伊。今年六月我就满24岁了，我住在蒙巴萨。我一辈子都在服药。我觉得当资金被停掉时对我来说真的很难，我去医院却找不到药。所以我去了，被告知下周来，下周来，而我还是找不到药。

00:56:18.720 --> 00:57:00.540
<v Jay>And I started getting this boils on my hand and legs and all over the body. So I started hiding out, and I wouldn't go to school. I started staying at home because I didn't want to be seen with the boils, the black spots that are still on my skin. I have to wear long sleeves, and Mombasa is very hot. I just hope they can be changed on that because personally, I've really suffered physically, mentally, and emotionally.
<v Jay>我开始在手上、腿上以及全身长这种疖子。所以我开始躲起来，不去上学。我开始呆在家里，因为我不想让别人看到那些疖子，以及至今仍留在我皮肤上的黑斑。我不得不穿长袖，而蒙巴萨非常热。我只希望他们能在这一点上有所改变，因为就我个人而言，我在身体上、精神上和情感上都遭受了真正的折磨。

00:57:00.540 --> 00:57:13.680
<v Ira Glass>Ike Sriskandarajah is one of the producers on our staff who called around to health workers scrambling in the wake of USAID cuts. He has this last story of people dealing with the abrupt cutoff of aid. That's Act Three, Two Daughters.
<v Ira Glass>艾克·斯里斯坎达拉贾是我们节目组的制作人之一，他曾致电那些在美国国际开发署削减资金的混乱中艰难支撑的医务人员。他带来了最后一个关于人们应对突如其来的援助中断的故事。这就是第三幕，两个女儿。

00:57:13.680 --> 00:57:44.140
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>While reporting this show, I heard many stories about chaos and confusion. But there were these two mothers and their daughters that I read about in a Washington Post story that really stuck with me. And I wanted to know how it's been going for them since. The first daughter, we'll call Sarah to protect her privacy. She's 15 and goes to a boarding school in western Kenya. She used to get her daily HIV tablets through a USAID-funded program, but they've been harder to find since the cuts.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>在报道这个节目时，我听到了许多关于混乱和迷茫的故事。但《华盛顿邮报》上的一篇报道中提到的两位母亲和她们的女儿，深深触动了我。我想知道她们最近过得怎么样。第一个女儿，为了保护隐私，我们叫她萨拉。她15岁，在肯尼亚西部的一所寄宿学校上学。她过去通过一个美国国际开发署资助的项目获得每日服用的艾滋病药片，但自预算削减以来，这些药变得更难找到了。

00:57:44.140 --> 00:58:12.100
<v Sarah>It's really terrifying. Medicine is a problem. I'm so worried because, now, accessing the medicine is a challenge. We have rumors that they are going to be selling at $500. I cannot afford the money. My stepmom can't afford right now. So it's only that it means that we are going to die. We are going to die without the medicine.
<v Sarah>这真的很可怕。药物是个问题。我很担心，因为现在获取药物成了挑战。有传言说它们要卖到500美元。我买不起。我的继母现在也买不起。所以这意味着我们要死了。我们没有药就会死。

00:58:12.100 --> 00:58:28.870
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>In the paper, she said one of her classmates was waking up screaming. Sarah said, mostly, she would just lie in bed, awake. When I called, she was down to two weeks of her medication. She was hoping her mother could find some and send them to her.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>她在报纸上说，她的一名同学醒来时在尖叫。萨拉说，大多数时候，她只是醒着躺在床上。我打电话时，她的药只够吃两个星期了。她希望她妈妈能找到一些寄给她。

00:58:28.870 --> 00:58:47.060
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>So I called her mom. And Sarah did not mention this, but her mother is a relentless force of nature when it comes to helping people with HIV. When I called, she was out on the street, trying to get the police to release an HIV-positive woman who they'd just arrested.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>于是我给她妈妈打了电话。萨拉没有提到这一点，但她的母亲在帮助艾滋病患者方面是一位不知疲倦的自然力量。我打电话时，她正在街上，试图让警察释放一个刚被抓的艾滋病毒呈阳性的女性。

00:58:47.060 --> 00:59:05.900
<v Mary>Yeah, I'm beside the police station. I'm trying to negotiate because one of them has to have medication today. So the time is up. I'm trying to speak with the officer in charge so that she can be released. That is the thing. But we can talk.
<v Mary>对，我在警察局旁边。我正在试着协商，因为其中一人今天必须吃药。时间快到了。我正试图跟负责人交谈，好让她被释放。就是这样。但我们可以谈谈。

00:59:05.900 --> 00:59:19.220
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Mary used to have a USAID-funded job at a clinic that paid her to do this work. Her paychecks ended in January, but she never punched out. It seems like she is everybody's emergency contact.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>玛丽过去在一家诊所有一份由美国国际开发署资助的工作，报酬就是让她做这项工作。她的薪水在1月停发了，但她从未停止工作。她似乎成了所有人的紧急联系人。

00:59:19.220 --> 00:59:33.240
<v Mary>I cannot switch off my phone at night because they usually call a lot, a lot. I did voluntary because it is my passion. And that's the reason why sometimes you find me, I have 20 kids. I have 15 kids.
<v Mary>我晚上不能关手机，因为他们通常会打很多很多电话。我是自愿做的，因为这是我的热情所在。这就是为什么有时你会发现，我有20个孩子。我有15个孩子。

00:59:33.240 --> 00:59:39.900
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>She's basically running a DIY orphanage out of her own apartment. She's taking care of 10 right now.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>她基本上是在自己的公寓里经营着一家自制孤儿院。她现在正照顾着10个孩子。

00:59:39.900 --> 00:59:50.160
<v Mary>I don't want them to suffer the way I suffer. They suffer. They really suffer. I'm not Mary, Mother of Jesus, but Mary. I don't want to see other people suffering.
<v Mary>我不想让他们承受我所受的苦。他们受苦。他们真的受苦。我不是耶稣的母亲玛丽，我只是玛丽。我不想看到别人受苦。

00:59:50.160 --> 01:00:40.530
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>She promised Sarah's mom that if anything happened to her, she would take care of her daughter. Then Sarah's mom was murdered. Mary keeps her word. So while Sarah was away at school, running out of medication, Mary was on it. She did the stuff you do when normal systems are broken. She worked her contacts, showed up at four different clinics. She found a friend who was willing to slip her a month's supply under the table. Then when Mary told that same friend that Sarah was sharing her meds with another girl at school who ran out, she was able to get a two-month supply. So Sarah is set, for now.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>她向萨拉的妈妈保证，如果她发生什么意外，她会照顾她的女儿。后来萨拉的妈妈遇害了。玛丽信守承诺。所以，当萨拉在外地上学、药物快用完时，玛丽一直在跟进。她做了在正常系统崩溃时你会做的事。她动用人脉，跑了四家不同的诊所。她找到一个愿意私下里塞给她一个月药量的朋友。后来，当玛丽告诉同一个朋友，萨拉正在和学校里另一个药用完的女孩分享药物时，她设法弄到了两个月的药量。所以萨拉暂时不用担心了。

01:00:40.530 --> 01:01:01.450
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>The other daughter I want to tell you about is Nyakewo. In The Washington Post story, Nyakewo told her mom, if she dies, her mom won't have to worry about feeding her. That was hard for Florence to hear. Both Florence and her daughter were HIV-positive. Florence had transmitted HIV to her daughter at birth.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>我要跟你讲的另一个女儿叫尼亚凯沃。在《华盛顿邮报》的报道中，尼亚凯沃告诉她妈妈，如果她死了，她妈妈就不用为喂饱她操心了。弗洛伦斯很难接受这一点。弗洛伦斯和她的女儿都是艾滋病毒阳性。弗洛伦斯在出生时将艾滋病毒传染给了女儿。

01:01:01.450 --> 01:01:16.720
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>When I reached her on the phone, she told me they were able to get their daily HIV pills. But the challenge came when Nyakewo got pregnant. They went searching for the drug that prevents the transmission of the virus from mother to child.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>当我通过电话联系到她时，她告诉我他们能够拿到每天服用的艾滋病药片。但挑战出现在尼亚凯沃怀孕的时候。他们开始寻找能够防止病毒从母亲传给孩子的药物。

01:01:16.720 --> 01:01:20.360
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>(SUBJECT) FLOREN I tried so much. I tried visiting different clinics.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>(SUBJECT) FLOREN 我尝试了非常多次。我试过去不同的诊所。

01:01:20.360 --> 01:01:22.390
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>How many clinics did you try?
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>你试了多少家诊所？

01:01:22.390 --> 01:01:23.080
<v Florence>Four.
<v Florence>四家。

01:01:23.080 --> 01:01:28.540
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Four. And they were all out of this medication?
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>四家。它们都没有这种药了吗？

01:01:28.540 --> 01:01:31.180
<v Florence>Yes, they are out of medication.
<v Florence>是的，它们没药了。

01:01:31.180 --> 01:01:58.440
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>I talked to one person who explained why. Apparently, the USAID-funded digital record system that a lot of clinics used had gone down. When that platform went down, health workers couldn't make new drug orders or tell if they had enough medicine for all their patients. So clinics started turning new patients away. Then Nyakewo fell deeper into depression. The father of the baby made her feel even worse.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>我跟一个人交谈过，他解释了原因。显然，许多诊所使用的由美国国际开发署资助的数字记录系统瘫痪了。当那个平台瘫痪时，医务人员就无法下达新的药物订单，也无法判断他们是否有足够的药物供所有病人使用。于是诊所开始将新病人拒之门外。然后尼亚凯沃陷入了更深的抑郁。孩子的父亲让她感觉更糟。

01:01:58.440 --> 01:02:25.170
<v Florence>The man said that she cannot give birth to a sick child because there is no medicine. And my daughter was there, trying to get rid of the child. Now, instead of getting rid, she decided to commit suicide. And it was not the first time for her to try to commit suicide. It was the third time.
<v Florence>那个男人说她不能生出一个生病的婴儿，因为没有药。我的女儿当时就在那里，试图打掉这个孩子。现在，她没有打掉，而是决定自杀。这已经不是她第一次尝试自杀。这是第三次。

01:02:25.170 --> 01:02:59.140
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Nyakewo died by suicide on May 1. She was 22 years old and three months pregnant. All three suicide attempts happened after USAID stopped so suddenly with no warning or back-up plans. Florence believes if her daughter had the HIV meds she needed to keep herself and her baby healthy, she'd still be alive.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>尼亚凯沃于5月1日自杀身亡。她22岁，怀孕三个月。这三次自杀未遂都发生在美国国际开发署毫无预警且没有备用计划的情况下突然停止援助之后。弗洛伦斯相信，如果她女儿拥有保持自己和婴儿健康所需的艾滋病药物，她现在还活着。

01:02:59.140 --> 01:03:13.930
<v Ira Glass>Ike Sriskandarajah. Katharine Houreld wrote the story where we first heard about Mary and Florence in The Washington Post.
<v Ira Glass>艾克·斯里斯坎达拉贾（Ike Sriskandarajah）。凯瑟琳·霍雷德（Katharine Houreld）在《华盛顿邮报》上写了这篇我们首次听到的关于玛丽和弗洛伦斯的故事。

01:03:13.930 --> 01:03:34.570
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Ike Sriskandarajah and edited by David Kestenbaum. The people who put together today's show include Phia Bennin, Jendayi Bonds, Mike Comite, Emmanuel Dzotsi, Audrey Fromson, Angela Gervasi, Miki Meek, Katherine Rae Mando, Stowe Nelson, Ryan Rumery, Lilly Sullivan, Frances Swanson, Christopher Swetala, and Julie Whitaker. Our managing editor is Sara Abdurrahman. Emanuele Berry is our executive editor.
<v Ira Glass>好的，我们今天的节目由艾克·斯里斯坎达拉贾制作，大卫·克斯滕鲍姆（David Kestenbaum）剪辑。参与制作今天节目的还有菲亚·本宁（Phia Bennin）、詹代·邦兹（Jendayi Bonds）、迈克·科米特（Mike Comite）、埃曼纽尔·佐齐（Emmanuel Dzotsi）、奥黛丽·弗罗姆森（Audrey Fromson）、安杰拉·杰瓦西（Angela Gervasi）、米基·米克（Miki Meek）、凯瑟琳·雷·曼多（Katherine Rae Mando）、斯托·尼尔森（Stowe Nelson）、瑞安·鲁梅里（Ryan Rumery）、莉莉·沙利文（Lilly Sullivan）、弗朗西丝·斯旺森（Frances Swanson）、克里斯托弗·斯韦塔拉（Christopher Swetala）和朱莉·惠特克（Julie Whitaker）。我们的执行主编是萨拉·阿卜杜拉曼（Sara Abdurrahman）。埃马纽埃莱·贝里（Emanuele Berry）是我们的执行主编。

01:03:34.570 --> 01:03:52.290
<v Ira Glass>Special thanks today to Oscar Rickett, Nicholas Kristof, Gabrielle Emanuel, Harerimana Ismail, Wendy Benzerga, Mark Dybul, Chris Makwindi, Sister Barbara Staley, Safiyah Riddle, and Lilian Lee. Our website, thisamericanlife.org, where you can stream our archive of over 850 episodes for absolutely free.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们要特别感谢奥斯卡·里克特（Oscar Rickett）、尼古拉斯·克里斯托夫（Nicholas Kristof）、加布里埃尔·埃马纽埃尔（Gabrielle Emanuel）、哈里里马纳·伊斯梅尔（Harerimana Ismail）、温迪·本泽加（Wendy Benzerga）、马克·迪布尔（Mark Dybul）、克里斯·马克温迪（Chris Makwindi）、芭芭拉·斯坦利修女（Sister Barbara Staley）、萨菲亚·里德尔（Safiyah Riddle）和莉莲·李（Lilian Lee）。我们的网站是 thisamericanlife.org，你可以在那里免费收听我们超过850集节目的资料库。

01:03:52.290 --> 01:04:07.500
<v Ira Glass>This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks, as always, to our program's co-founder, Mr. Torey Malatia. Torey knows all the tricks of hard-hitting professional journalism-- who, what, where, when?
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》由公共广播交易所（PRX）分发给公共广播电台。一如既往地感谢我们节目的联合创始人托雷·马拉蒂亚（Torey Malatia）先生。托雷深谙有深度、有冲击力的专业新闻的所有诀窍——谁、什么、哪里、什么时候？

01:04:07.500 --> 01:04:09.810
<v Navyn Salem>The next obvious question is, why?
<v Navyn Salem>接下来的显而易见的问题是，为什么？

01:04:09.810 --> 01:04:14.810
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。下周将带着更多《美国生活》的故事回归。
